('cat:smart-home','Quote analysis & comparison',9,'A defensible smart-home quote separates hardware (~55%), labour/programming (~40%) and infrastructure rather than lumping a ''smart home package''. Line items to demand: controller/processor with named model (Control4 EA/CORE, Crestron, KNX line/IP coupler + ETS programming, Lutron HomeWorks, Loxone Miniserver) - ''smart controller'' with no model is a red flag; gateways/bridges per protocol (Zigbee/Z-Wave hub, KNX IP gateway, Matter controller); lighting load/dimmer modules quoted PER CIRCUIT with dimmable distinguished from switched and dimming type stated (leading/trailing edge, 0-10V, DALI); number of lighting scenes per room; keypads/touch panels by count and size (7in/10in, in-wall vs tabletop); AV zones and HVAC zones (one interface per FCU/AC); motorized curtains/blinds by track count with motor brand (Somfy/Lutron) and whether tracks are included; access/intercom (flag SIRA scope if CCTV/access added); network rack, managed PoE switch (port count), Wi-Fi 6 APs (count), UPS; structured cabling Cat6/Cat6A drop count, fibre backbone, conduit, patch panel, certified/tested drops; app/remote-access licensing (flag recurring); explicit programming hours; warranty years and AMC. Compare by normalising to the same room list and same count of circuits/zones/panels/drops; check protocol parity (a wired-KNX and wireless-Zigbee quote at the same price are not equivalent), and check what is ''by others'' (cabling, conduit, DB modification). The cheapest corner cut, most-often-first: structured cabling (a ''wireless throughout'' new-build saved on wiring and shifted risk to battery/mesh endpoints), then cable downgrade (Cat5e/unbranded), under-provisioned APs (one router for a multi-floor villa), token programming hours, omitted commissioning/testing, no surge/UPS, warranty/AMC excluded, and cloud-only control with no local fallback.'),
('cat:smart-home','Customer intake & scoping questions',9,'1. Property type and stage: new-build/off-plan or retrofit of an occupied home? Wired is only easy pre-plaster. 2. Developer and community (Emaar/Nakheel/DAMAC/freehold)? Drives the NOC/fit-out approval path. 3. Built-up area and layout: floors, bedrooms, full room list (drives AP count, zones, cable drops). 4. Which subsystems: lighting, shading/curtains, HVAC, AV/multiroom audio, CCTV/access, intercom, irrigation, pool, gate, ranked must-have vs nice-to-have? 5. Lighting scope: how many circuits, which need dimming, and how many scenes per room? 6. AV expectations: audio zones, dedicated cinema, video distribution? 7. HVAC type: central/VRF, splits or FCUs, and how many independently controlled zones? 8. Security in scope? If CCTV/access control is included, SIRA applies, flag early. 9. Control preferences: wall keypads, touch panels, phone app, voice (Alexa/Google/Siri)? 10. Internet and networking: current ISP/router, willingness to install structured cabling and a rack, device count? 11. Budget band and whether they value vendor flexibility (KNX) or turnkey UX (Control4)? 12. Resilience: must it keep working offline during an internet outage? 13. Energy/solar/EV: any DEWA Shams Dubai PV, EV charger or energy-monitoring intent? 14. Aftercare: AMC wanted, remote support, and acceptable warranty term?'),
('cat:smart-home','Regulations, licensing & approvals',8,'A smart-home/ELV company needs a valid DED (Dubai Economy and Tourism) trade licence under an electrical/electromechanical/low-current or IT-systems activity. Any work touching the mains installation or distribution board must be done under a DEWA-enrolled electrical contractor; DEWA enrolment requires a valid DED licence and a sponsored graduate electrical engineer (>=1 year experience) supervising works per DEWA Regulations. Pure ELV automation that does not modify the DB may not itself trigger DEWA enrolment - confirm for the specific scope. TDRA equipment type approval is mandatory (verified, official TDRA) for any device with an RF feature (Wi-Fi, Zigbee, Z-Wave, Thread/Matter, Bluetooth, NFC/RFID): equipment must be registered/type-approved before sale, distribution or use; importers need TDRA Dealer Registration; three risk levels carry SLAs (Level 1 ~1 day, Level 2 ~5 days, Level 3 ~10 days) with ISO/IEC 17025 ILAC-accredited test reports. Grey-market wireless gear is a red flag; verify models against the TDRA Approved Equipment list. SIRA applies the moment CCTV or access control is added - the installer must be SIRA-approved with consultant design and a SIRA NOC; a pure-automation firm without SIRA cannot legally deliver that part. For fire-event automation, Dubai Civil Defence governs the certified fire system (NFPA 72-aligned, DCD-approved contractor, annual AMC); the smart system interfaces via a single dedicated interface panel (typically Modbus RTU over RS-485) and must not replace or compromise the fire system; villas may fall under the MOI Hassantuk scheme. DEWA Shams Dubai (Council Resolution 46 of 2014) governs grid-connected rooftop PV (DRRG-enrolled contractor, 1 MW cap). There is no confirmed public consumer DEWA meter API - do not promise live DEWA-meter integration. Apartment/villa fit-out changes require a DM fit-out permit or developer NOC (e.g. Emaar Community Portal) by a registered contractor.'),
('cat:smart-home','Standards, materials & specifications',8,'Protocols and tradeoffs. KNX: wired twisted-pair bus (also RF/IP), open standard, decentralised (no single point of failure, each device intelligent), highly reliable/scalable and vendor-interoperable, best for new-build villas on a 10-15 year horizon, but higher cost/time and needs cabling pre-plaster plus ETS programming. Zigbee: wireless 2.4 GHz mesh, low-power, large ecosystem, but 2.4 GHz congestion and battery maintenance. Z-Wave: sub-GHz mesh, better range/penetration, lower bandwidth. Wi-Fi: high bandwidth for cameras/streaming but network load and cloud dependency. Thread/Matter: Thread mesh plus the Matter interoperability layer, still maturing. Design rule: a hybrid wins - a wired backbone (KNX or Control4 with structured cabling) plus wireless endpoints where cabling is infeasible. Ecosystem positioning: KNX (vendor independence/longevity), Control4 (turnkey owner-friendly AV UX), Crestron (high-end/custom), Lutron (premium lighting and shading), Loxone (value all-in-one). Cabling/infrastructure: Cat6/Cat6A home runs to a central rack (no daisy-chaining), Cat6A recommended for AP/camera/PoE backbone and future 10G; fibre backbone for large villas; dedicated KNX bus cable pulled pre-plaster; deliverables include patch panel, labelling and certified/tested drops; network rack with managed PoE switch, UPS and surge protection. Fire interface via a dedicated interface panel/Modbus RS-485 to the DCD panel. Codes/standards to reference: DEWA Regulations for Electrical Installations (mains/DB work), NFPA 72 / UAE Fire and Life Safety Code (fire interface), SIRA technical standards (CCTV/access), TDRA type approval (all wireless), ISO/IEC 17025-accredited test reports for type approval.'),
('cat:smart-home','Pricing benchmarks & red flags',7,'Indicative AED benchmarks (UAE integrator/secondary sources, 2025-2026, directional only): budget guideline roughly 4-7% of total fit-out cost for a properly engineered automation layer; 3-4BR villa mid-range KNX (lighting, climate, basic security) ~AED 80,000-150,000; 4BR (lighting, shading, climate, security) ~AED 80,000-160,000; mid-range with multi-zone audio plus security integration ~AED 200,000-500,000; 6BR premium full Control4 (whole-home AV, cinema, pool, irrigation) ~AED 220,000-450,000; palace-grade Crestron with custom UI ~AED 800,000-3,000,000+. Labour benchmarks (international, convert with judgement): integration/programming ~USD 80-150/hr, low-voltage cabling/camera tech ~USD 60-90/hr. Red flags: a price far below the 4-7%-of-fit-out band (scope or cabling missing); no named brands/models for controller, panels or dimmers; ''wireless throughout'' on a new-build (corner cut = infrastructure/future-proofing); Cat5e/unbranded cable or no certification; one router and no PoE APs for a multi-floor home; programming hours absent or token (change-order trap); no surge/UPS on the rack and controllers; no warranty/AMC or warranty under 1 year/parts-only; cloud-only control with no local fallback; imported wireless gear with no TDRA approval; CCTV/access included but installer not SIRA-approved (illegal); fire-event automation promised without a DCD-approved contractor and interface panel; ''live DEWA meter integration'' promised without a verifiable data path (likely overstated).'),
('cat:waterproofing','Quote analysis & comparison',9,'A compliant Dubai waterproofing quote is line-itemed by substrate/zone, exposing the full system rather than ''supply and apply membrane''. Demand: measured area m2 per zone (not lump-sum) - roof, podium, basement raft, retaining wall, each bathroom/wet area, water tank, planter, pool; system type named explicitly (liquid-applied PU/acrylic, cementitious 1- or 2-component, bituminous torch-on APP or SBS, self-adhesive bitumen, PVC/TPO/HDPE/EPDM sheet, or crystalline); number of coats (liquid) or plies (sheet) - PU/cementitious wet areas typically 2-3 coats, torch-on roofs typically 2 layers; DFT in microns/mm for liquid and membrane thickness mm for sheet (commonly 3-4 mm torch-on, 1.2-2.0 mm PVC/TPO, 1.5-2.0 mm HDPE); the manufacturer''s matched primer (bitumen primer for torch-on, epoxy/PU for liquid on concrete); reinforcement mesh/fleece at coves, joints, drains and pipe penetrations; mortar fillet/coving at wall-floor junctions; surface prep (grinding/shot-blasting, crack repair, laitance removal, moisture check, fall-to-drain); protection layer (screed, board, geotextile or drainage board for buried/podium/planter); flood/ponding test 24-48h with documented results; warranty (years, and material-only vs full system/applicator); GRP lining where specified; drainage detail. Compare apples-to-apples by normalising to AED/m2 by zone AND to total dry build-up (coats x DFT, or membrane mm + protection); the cheaper-per-m2 number is meaningless without the build-up. Corner cuts (almost always in invisible layers the client cannot see after completion): fewer coats/plies; lower DFT/thinner membrane; skipped primer (adhesion failure); skipped protection screed/board (membrane punctured at backfill); skipped flood test; no mesh/fillet at coves and penetrations; a cheaper non-DCL membrane substituted as ''equivalent''; no surface-prep line; lump-sum hiding a quantity shortfall; and a ''material-only'' warranty disguised as a 10-year warranty.'),
('cat:waterproofing','Standards, materials & specifications',8,'Standards cited for UAE waterproofing (confirm specific citations against the Code PDF before customer-facing use): EN 13707 (reinforced bituminous roof sheets); ASTM D6134 (self-adhered bituminous membranes); ASTM C836 (cold liquid-applied elastomeric PU/bituminous); plus ASTM D412/D751 (tensile/elongation), ASTM D5957 (ponding/flood test guide), ASTM E1643/E1745 (under-slab vapour barriers), ASTM D6878 (TPO), ASTM D4434 (PVC), BS 8102 (below-ground tanking reference), BS 8204 (screeds), EN 1504 (concrete repair), DIN 18195/18533 (building waterproofing). System selection by substrate: bathroom/wet area - 2-3 coats flexible cementitious or PU with mesh at coves and penetrations, mortar fillet, then 24-48h flood test before tiling; flat roof/terrace (exposed) - UV-resistant 4 mm APP torch-on (often 2 layers) or 2-component PU with aliphatic UV topcoat, laid to fall; podium/trafficable deck - heavy-duty PU or torch-on plus protection screed and drainage board; basement raft/retaining wall - full tanking per BS 8102 (pre-applied HDPE/bituminous sheet, post-applied bituminous/PU, or crystalline) with protection and drainage board, designed for negative-side hydrostatic and saline groundwater; potable water tank - 2-component flexible cementitious certified for potable contact and/or GRP, with flood test; swimming pool - 2-component cementitious or PVC sheet liner; planter - root-resistant membrane plus drainage board; lift pits/no-future-access structures - crystalline (self-healing). Materials/brands (verify each SKU''s current DCL certificate): Sika (frequently mandated on Emaar - Sikalastic PU, Sikaplan PVC, SikaProof), MBCC/Master Builders Solutions MasterSeal, Fosroc (Proofex, Nitoproof), Mapei (Mapelastic), Drizoro (Maxseal/Maxelastic), and DCL-certified local makers. Indicative service life: bituminous with protection 10-15 yr; exposed PU 5-10 yr; cementitious 3-7 yr; protected PVC/TPO/HDPE sheet 15-25 yr.'),
('cat:painting','Authoritative sources & references',4,'Jotun Paints UAE — https://www.jotun.com/
Dulux / AkzoNobel Middle East — https://www.dulux.ae/
National Paints — https://www.nationalpaints.com/
Caparol Arabia — https://www.caparol.ae/
Hempel (protective coatings) — https://www.hempel.com/
