('cat:waterproofing','Pricing benchmarks & red flags',7,'Indicative AED/m2 (supply plus apply, labour included; trade-sourced ranges that vary with prep, access, thickness and warranty): overall UAE ~AED 40-175/m2; cementitious ~AED 40-70/m2; torch-on bitumen ~AED 45-75/m2; roof (system-dependent) ~AED 45-150/m2; basement from ~AED 80/m2 (basic) up to AED 500+/m2 for full structural tanking/remediation; material-only membrane rolls (1 m x 10 m, 4 mm) ~AED 130-246/roll. Where corners get cut and the red flags: fewer coats/plies than spec (the single most common cut - 1 coat instead of 2-3 leaves pinholes in corners and behind pipes); lower DFT or thinner membrane (0.8-1.0 mm where 1.5-2.0 mm is needed, or 3 mm torch-on where 4 mm is specified) - demand DFT in microns/mm; skipping the matched primer (adhesion failure from day one); skipping the protection screed/board on buried/podium/planter work (membrane punctured at backfill); skipping the flood/ponding test and its documentation (the verification step before tiling/covering); no mesh reinforcement or mortar fillet at coves and penetrations; a cheaper non-DCL membrane substituted as ''equivalent'' with no Certificate of Conformity offered; no surface-prep line (prep is 30-40% of a good job); lump-sum instead of measured m2 by zone (hides quantity shortfall); and a material-only warranty disguised as a 10-year warranty (confirm it covers applicator workmanship). The corner-cutting quote is meaningfully cheaper per m2 AND silent on at least one of coat count, DFT/thickness, primer, mesh/fillet, protection layer, flood test, DCL certificate, or warranty scope.'),
('cat:aluminium-glass','Standards, materials & specifications',8,'Frames: architectural aluminium alloy 6063-T5/T6 extrusions; finishes are powder-coating (commonly to Qualicoat/AAMA quality, typical 60+ microns) or anodising (typical 15-25 microns), with thermal-break systems using polyamide strips for energy performance. Glass: float/annealed, heat-strengthened, fully tempered (toughened) and laminated (PVB interlayer 0.38/0.76/1.52mm); ''safety glass'' means tempered or laminated and is required for doors, low-level glazing, balustrades and overhead glazing. Insulated Glass Units (IGUs) for double glazing reduce U-value (good units around 1.2-1.8 W/m2K) and SHGC; Low-E coatings cut solar heat gain for Al Sa''fat/Estidama compliance. Curtain wall/structural glazing relies on structural silicone and weather seals with wind-load engineering. Performance vocabulary: U-value (thermal transmittance, W/m2K), SHGC/g-value, visible light transmittance, acoustic Rw (dB), air/water/wind-load ratings. Fire-rated glazing is classified by integrity/insulation (EI 30/60/90/120) tested to EN standards under DCD supervision. UAE building products are subject to ESMA/MoIAT ECAS conformity. Reputable brands include Guardian, Saint-Gobain, Emirates Glass for glass; Technal, Schueco, Reynaers, Alupco, Gulf Extrusions for systems; sealants by Dow, Sika.'),
('cat:flooring','Regulations, licensing & approvals',8,'Flooring is typically delivered as part of fit-out or construction, requiring the relevant permit by location: Dubai Municipality (DM) Building Permit System for mainland projects, Dubai Development Authority (DDA) fit-out permit in its districts, or Trakhees for Nakheel/free-zone areas; work should be by a DM/DDA-licensed contractor with drawings by a licensed consultant. The Dubai Building Code and DM guidelines apply to safe flooring (slip resistance in wet/public areas is a compliance matter, not just a preference). Dubai Civil Defence (DCD) involvement arises where flooring affects fire compartmentation, escape routes, or where resin/coating fire characteristics matter in specific occupancies; obtain NOCs as required. Building/finishing products such as tiles, adhesives and resin systems fall under ESMA/MoIAT conformity (ECAS) and should carry test data (slip, abrasion, VOC for resins). For major fit-outs affecting 50%+ of built-up area, Al Sa''fat 2.0 Silver sustainability compliance can be triggered, which can influence material VOC and sourcing. Practical workflow: licensed contractor prepares layout/finishes drawings, secures fit-out/building permit (DM/DDA/Trakhees), confirms any DCD NOC, installs per spec, then inspection/completion sign-off. Confirm the governing authority by the project''s exact location before quoting timelines.'),
('cat:joinery-kitchens','Quote analysis & comparison',9,'A complete joinery/kitchen quote should itemise per unit: carcass (board type & thickness — e.g. 18mm MR-MDF or MR particleboard, with edge-banding type), doors/fronts (finish: melamine/HPL laminate, foil/PVC, acrylic, lacquer/PU, or wood veneer; gloss vs matt), worktop (laminate, quartz/engineered stone, granite, solid surface, with thickness and brand), backsplash, hardware (brand and model — Blum or Hettich hinges/runners, soft-close, lift systems; or budget Grass/Hi-Tech/M5), internal accessories (cutlery trays, pull-outs, corner units), handles, plinth, and installation. Differentiators with units: board grade and E1 formaldehyde class; MR (moisture-resistant) vs standard for wet/humid areas; finish type (veneer vs laminate vs lacquer changes price sharply); hardware brand (genuine Blum/Hettich vs unbranded); worktop material & mm; soft-close on all vs some. Strong quote: names board grade/E1, finish per door, exact hardware brand/model, worktop material, and edge-banding (laser/PUR vs PVC). Corner-cutting: carcass switched to non-MR or thinner board; veneer ''look'' that is actually printed foil; ''Blum-type'' rather than genuine Blum; soft-close only on doors not drawers; cheap PVC edging that peels in heat. Tricks: sample shown in premium finish then downgraded; worktop ''allowance'' not specified; supply-only vs supply-and-install; delivery/installation/site-measure excluded; VAT excluded.'),
('cat:swimming-pools','Quote analysis & comparison',9,'A credible Dubai pool quote itemises rather than bundling a ''turnkey'' figure. Civil/shell: excavation and spoil removal (contingency for rock/caliche); shell type and method (reinforced concrete gunite/shotcrete vs poured, fiberglass shell, or vinyl liner); rebar grade and spacing; shell thickness (concrete walls/floor typically 200-250 mm - silence is a red flag); waterproofing membrane named as a separate line (never ''included''); screed; coping per linear metre; deck/surround (DM requires deck area >= pool area). Finish: tiling grade (ceramic/porcelain/glass mosaic/marble plaster) per m2; DM mandates light-colour smooth ceramic floor. Hydraulics: filtration type and flow rate m3/h sized to volume; pumps with brand/kW (DM requires two pumps per pool, turnover <=4 hours); skimmers, min 2 base drains, return inlets (1 per 45 m3); hair strainer; balance/surge tank (mandatory for overflow/infinity/deck-level). Treatment: chlorination method (DM bans manual dosing for public pools; private may use a feeder or auto system); ORP-pH controller. Comfort: heat pump and/or chiller (Dubai needs summer cooling too). Electrical: low-voltage underwater lights (<=50 LUX, RCD per circuit). Safety: perimeter fence >=1.2 m with self-closing/self-latching gate, depth markers, bilingual signage, ladders/steps. Soft: DM/DEWA/Civil Defence approvals, warranty split (structural 5-10 yr vs equipment 1-2 yr), commissioning/first fill, AMC. Corner cuts and consequences: no waterproofing line (leaks, worst omission); thin shell/light rebar (cracking in saline ground); undersized/single pump (fails the 4-hr turnover); no balance tank on an infinity/deck-level pool; manual dosing on a public pool (non-compliant); generic unbranded equipment; approvals excluded; no deck/fence. Normalise to the same volume, equipment tier and finish; a bid 25-40% below the cluster has a missing line, not a deal.'),
('cat:waterproofing','Authoritative sources & references',4,'Dubai Building Code 2021 (official PDF, Dubai Municipality) — https://www.dm.gov.ae/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/Dubai%20Building%20Code_English_2021%20Edition_compressed.pdf
