('cat:aluminium-glass','Pricing benchmarks & red flags',7,'Indicative UAE prices (verify per project; exclude VAT 5% unless stated): standard aluminium windows/doors with single glazing often run roughly AED 250-500/m2; double-glazed/thermally-broken systems higher; curtain wall/structural glazing typically AED 600-1,500+/m2 depending on glass make-up, finish and access; high-spec anodised/Low-E IGU facades on towers can exceed AED 1,800/m2. Key price drivers: glass make-up (single vs IGU, Low-E, laminated), thermal break, profile depth and wall thickness, finish (PVDF/anodised adds ~8-12%), system brand vs generic, height/access (scaffold/crane), wind-load engineering, and quantity. Red flags: price far below market usually means single glazing substituted for double, thinner or annealed glass instead of tempered/laminated, no thermal break, generic profiles below required wall thickness, cheap gaskets/sealant, and missing DCD/structural-glazing certificates. Also watch for supply-only disguised as installed, excluded scaffolding/crane, omitted VAT, ''standard glass'' with no thickness/type stated, no U-value, and no finish microns or warranty. A credible quote states alloy, profile series, finish microns, glass make-up with thicknesses, U-value, thermal break and warranty.'),
('cat:flooring','Standards, materials & specifications',8,'Tiles: porcelain (water absorption <0.5%, dense, ideal for UAE wet areas) vs ceramic; abrasion graded by PEI 1-5 (PEI 3+ for floors, 4-5 for heavy/commercial traffic); slip resistance by DIN 51130 R-ratings R9-R13 (R10/R11+ for wet areas) and COF (≥0.5 considered safe wet); rectified edges allow thin joints; common formats 600x600/600x1200mm at 8-12mm. Adhesives/grouts to EN 12004 (C1 normal, C2 improved for porcelain/stone, with optional flexibility/fast-set classes) and EN 13888 for grouts, including 2-part epoxy grout (e.g. R2T/RG class) for chemical/water resistance. Substrate prep uses cementitious screed and self-levelling compound; outdoor/UAE-heat applications need high-polymer, waterproof adhesive (surfaces can exceed 60C). Wood: engineered vs solid parquet vs laminate; laminate durability by AC rating (AC3 residential to AC5 heavy commercial), with top-layer/veneer thickness (mm) key for engineered wood. Epoxy/resin: self-levelling or screed systems, primer + body + topcoat, typical 1-3mm (up to 5mm industrial mortar), with anti-slip aggregate and coving options; check VOC/Al Sa''fat. Vocabulary: PEI, R-rating/COF, water absorption, AC rating, microns/mm thickness, C2/EN 12004. Brands: Mapei, Sika, Weber, Laticrete, Sodamco (adhesives/resin); Quick-Step/Egger (laminate).'),
('cat:joinery-kitchens','Customer intake & scoping questions',9,'1) What item(s) — kitchen, wardrobes, vanity, TV/media unit, reception desk, full villa joinery? 2) Measurements or a site visit — running metres of cabinetry and ceiling height (for tall units)? 3) Is it a kitchen layout (L/U/island/galley) and do they have a plan? 4) Carcass preference: standard or moisture-resistant (MR) board, and any E1 low-formaldehyde requirement? 5) Door/front finish: laminate/HPL, melamine, foil/PVC, acrylic, lacquer/PU, or natural wood veneer — and gloss or matt? 6) Worktop material and thickness: laminate, quartz/engineered stone, granite, or solid surface (and brand)? 7) Hardware grade: genuine Blum or Hettich, or budget brand — soft-close on doors and drawers? 8) Internal accessories: pull-outs, cutlery inserts, corner units, lighting? 9) Appliances — integrated or freestanding, and are appliance cut-outs needed? 10) Colour/RAL or reference images. 11) Site type (villa, apartment, office) and any building NOC/working-hours rules. 12) Timeline and required completion date. 13) Budget range. 14) Supply-only or supply-and-install, including site measurement, delivery and old-unit removal?'),
('cat:swimming-pools','Standards, materials & specifications',8,'Verified from DM-HSD-GU34-ASPP2 V4.0. Hydraulics: water turnover must not exceed 4 hours (required flow m3/h = volume m3 / 4); 1 inlet per 45 m3; at least 2 base outlet drains; 2 pumps per pool with an upstream hair strainer; 2 skimmers per pool where used; recognised filters are diatomaceous earth, rapid sand and high-rate sand (>=1 private, >=2 public). Disinfection: public pools need automatic free-chlorine/bromine dosing (manual not accepted); private may use a feeder or auto system; chlorine-gas safety required (Trakhees: gas mask in an unlocked cabinet outside the chlorine room, no tablets in the skimmer). Water quality (from GU81/Trakhees, exact ppm/pH conflict across sources - re-verify the live GU81 PDF and present the tighter band as the set-point): free chlorine commonly cited 1-2 ppm (some sources 1-3 ppm); pH target 7.4-7.6 (acceptable 7.2-7.8); water must be clear with the deepest point visible. Testing frequency (Trakhees LD-RC-G10): daily chlorine/pH/temperature logbook, every 15 days hardness/alkalinity/cyanuric acid, microbiological every 2 months. Structural/geometry: floor slope not less than 1:3, deck slope 1:40 away from the pool, deck area >= pool area; ladders >=1 per 30 m circumference; max step riser 250 mm, min tread 280 mm; diving needs >=2.5 m depth and >=5 m head space; low-voltage underwater lighting <=50 LUX with RCD per circuit; light-colour smooth ceramic floor. Concrete/gunite shell with cementitious or sheet waterproofing membrane and glass-mosaic/porcelain finish is the premium Dubai standard; fiberglass is faster/size-limited; vinyl liner is rare. Common equipment brands serviced locally: Pentair, Hayward, AstralPool/Fluidra, Emaux, Kripsol, Waterco, Jandy.'),
('cat:false-ceiling','Customer intake & scoping questions',9,'1) Ceiling type: plain gypsum, plasterboard, gypsum tiles/grid (e.g. 600x600), bulkheads/coves, or partitions? 2) Area in m2 (ceiling) and linear metres/height for partitions, plus room count? 3) Property/space type: villa, apartment, office, retail, clinic, kitchen, plant room? 4) Any fire-rating requirement (escape routes, stairwells, service cores, between tenancies) and how many minutes (60/90/120)? 5) Wet or humid areas (bathroom, kitchen, external soffit) needing moisture-resistant board? 6) Acoustic requirement between rooms (target Rw dB) and insulation needed? 7) Board brand/type preference (Knauf, Gyproc, USG) and thickness? 8) Partition: full-height to slab or to ceiling only; single or double board per face; stud size? 9) Finishes: skim/putty, paint, or board only; any curves/coves/lighting coffers? 10) Services in ceiling: AC grilles, lights, sprinklers, access panels, sound insulation? 11) Compliance: DCD fire NOC, DM/DDA fit-out approval needed? 12) Is this supply & install or supply-only; demolition of existing? 13) Site condition, timeline, and after-hours work limits? 14) Budget range and whether VAT is shown separately?'),
('cat:tiling-marble','Quote analysis & comparison',9,'A complete tiling & marble quote shows, per item: stone/material type and origin (e.g. Italian Carrara, Persian, Spanish; granite colour), format (slab vs tile) and size, thickness (mm), finish (polished/honed/leather/flamed), edge profile, substrate prep (screed/self-levelling, slurry bond coat), fixing method (cement mortar/adhesive class or epoxy), grouting/pointing, sealing/impregnation, skirting/risers/nosing, polishing/crystallisation, cutting/fabrication, and supply & install vs supply-only. Differentiating spec parameters (with units): stone origin/grade, thickness (e.g. 18/20mm slabs, 20mm counters, 30mm floors), finish type, water absorption/porosity, and for ceramic/porcelain the PEI and slip R-rating. A strong quote names origin, thickness, finish, adhesive/mortar class (EN 12004 C2 for stone) and includes sealing and polishing; an underpriced one cuts corners with thinner stone (e.g. 15-16mm where 18-20mm specified), lower-grade/B-grade or filled stone, cheaper cement mortar instead of proper C2 adhesive or stone-safe (non-staining white) adhesive, no penetrating sealer (marble stains/etches), reduced/skipped levelling, and skipped final polish/crystallisation. Highlight to customers: stone origin and thickness, finish, sealing inclusion, adhesive/mortar suitability for stone, and edge/skirting work. Common tricks: supply-only quoted like installed, sealing/polishing excluded, skirting and special cuts excluded, VAT and wastage hidden, off-origin substitution (''Italian look''), and vague ''marble work'' with no thickness, origin or finish.'),
('cat:joinery-kitchens','Pricing benchmarks & red flags',7,'Indicative only — joinery is usually priced per running/linear metre or per piece and varies widely with finish and hardware. Budget melamine/foil kitchens with unbranded hardware sit at the low end; mid-range laminate/acrylic with genuine Blum/Hettich are higher; lacquer (PU) and natural veneer with quartz/stone worktops and full soft-close are premium. Worktops are a major swing: laminate cheapest, then solid surface, granite, and engineered quartz. Drivers: linear metres, board grade (MR vs standard), finish type (lacquer/veneer most expensive), hardware brand, worktop material/thickness, internal accessories, and site complexity. Add 5% VAT and delivery/installation. Red flags: price far below peers usually means non-MR or thinner carcass, foil masquerading as veneer, ''Blum-type'' unbranded hardware, soft-close only on doors, or cheap PVC edge-banding prone to peeling in UAE heat/humidity; worktop quoted as an unspecified ''allowance''; sample finish downgraded after order; supply-only quoted against a competitor''s supply-and-install; no E1/test documentation; no site measurement or installation included.'),
('cat:signage','Standards, materials & specifications',8,'Core materials: acrylic (cast/extruded, 3-5mm, for faces and built-up letters), ACP/aluminium composite panel (3-4mm, for backing/cladding — facade use should be fire-rated, e.g. A2/FR grade given UAE facade-fire regulations), stainless steel (brushed/mirror, for premium letters), galvanised/aluminium frames and raceways, foam/PVC and flex/vinyl for budget or temporary signs. Illumination: LED modules (front-lit through acrylic face, halo/back-lit for a glow behind letters, or edge-lit), with appropriate transformers/drivers — outdoor LEDs and power supplies should be IP-rated (e.g. IP65) for weather and heat resistance. Fabrication: CNC routing, laser cutting, and channel-letter bending. Structural and wind-load: large or high-mounted signs require engineered fixings and wind-load calculations per the structural certification Dubai Municipality demands; electrical works follow DEWA regulations. Approval drawings must be to scale with dimensions and a mock-up. Vocabulary to capture in specs: material + thickness, letter height/return depth, illumination type, LED brand and IP rating, fixing/mounting method, and fire-rating of ACP. Specify exact colours by Pantone/RAL to match brand artwork.'),
('cat:cctv-security','Closed-case lesson (RFQ-bf2f91d7-55d2-4937-8311-55184205c57e)',60,'An RFQ cancelled within minutes of creation — before any supplier engagement — likely indicates an accidental submission, duplicate entry, or an immediate change in scope; a brief mandatory confirmation step or short cooling-off window before broadcasting to suppliers would reduce noise in the pipeline.'),
('cat:swimming-pools','Pricing benchmarks & red flags',7,'Indicative AED pricing (2026 secondary sources, directional not authoritative). Construction overall ~AED 45,000 (small fiberglass) to AED 250,000+, with many sources citing AED 90,000-350,000 for typical residential builds depending on size, design and features. Concrete: from ~AED 80,000, typical AED 70,000-250,000, roughly AED 400-600/sq ft. Fiberglass: from ~AED 45,000, typical AED 60,000-230,000, roughly AED 300-500/sq ft. Standard villa pool (~8 m x 4 m): AED 90,000-140,000. Build time: concrete 6-10 weeks, fiberglass 3-5 weeks, plus approvals. Maintenance: per-visit cleaning AED 150-300; monthly AMC from ~AED 500 (small) to AED 800-1,200 (medium weekly) to AED 1,500+ (large); annual contract commonly AED 3,000-6,000, up to AED 4,000-10,000 for larger pools; AMC covers cleaning, water-chemistry balancing, equipment checks, leak/algae checks - weekly visits recommended for Dubai conditions. Utilities: DEWA villa activation refundable deposit ~AED 4,000 plus activation fee ~AED 155; first fill by mains or tanker. Pricing red flags: a bid 25-40% below the comparable cluster (a line item is missing - usually waterproofing, shell thickness/rebar, second pump/filter, or balance tank); a single lump-sum ''turnkey'' price with no itemisation; equipment with no brand/model/kW/flow; no approvals scope; no warranty split (structural vs equipment); no AMC; and high-pressure ''limited-time'' discounting.'),
('cat:false-ceiling','Regulations, licensing & approvals',8,'Interior gypsum ceiling/partition work is part of fit-out, which requires a permit from the relevant authority by location: Dubai Municipality (DM) via its Building Permit System for mainland, Dubai Development Authority (DDA) for its free-zone districts (mandatory fit-out permit and completion submission), or Trakhees for Nakheel/free-zone areas. Work must be carried out by a DM/DDA-licensed contractor; drawings are submitted by a licensed consultant. Dubai Civil Defence (DCD) approval/NOC is required where fire-rated ceilings or partitions form part of life-safety: fire-rated assemblies are mandated for escape routes, stairwells, plant/electrical rooms, between separate tenancies and in many commercial buildings above a set height, with rated systems achieving 60-120 minutes; DCD references the UAE Fire and Life Safety Code of Practice. Gypsum board products fall under ESMA/MoIAT conformity (ECAS) and should carry manufacturer fire-rating certificates and test reports for the specific system used. Practical workflow: appoint licensed contractor/consultant, prepare layout and reflected-ceiling drawings, obtain DCD NOC for fire-rated elements, secure fit-out permit (DM/DDA/Trakhees), build to the certified system, then inspection and completion sign-off. Major fit-outs affecting 50%+ of built-up area can trigger full Al Sa''fat 2.0 Silver compliance.'),
('cat:tiling-marble','Customer intake & scoping questions',9,'1) Material: natural marble, granite, limestone, travertine, onyx, engineered/quartz stone, or porcelain tile? 2) Application: floor, wall cladding, stairs/risers, countertop/vanity, skirting, feature wall, external? 3) Area in m2 and linear metres (skirting/edges/stairs), plus piece sizes? 4) Property/space type: villa, apartment, hotel/lobby, retail, bathroom, kitchen? 5) Stone origin/grade preference (Italian, Turkish, Spanish, Indian granite) and any specific name/colour? 6) Format and thickness: slab vs tile, required thickness (mm) for floor vs counter? 7) Finish: polished, honed, leathered, flamed (granite), brushed; and edge profile (bullnose, bevel, mitred)? 8) Slip/wet-area needs (bathroom, pool, stairs) and any slip rating? 9) Sealing/impregnation required (marble/limestone stain and etch protection)? 10) Substrate condition: bare slab, screed, old finish - removal/levelling needed? 11) Pattern/layout, book-matching, vein direction, grout colour? 12) Compliance/approvals (DM/DDA fit-out) and any heritage/landlord rules? 13) Supply & install or supply-only; who supplies stone; site access for heavy slabs? 14) Timeline, working-hours limits, budget range, and VAT shown separately?'),
('cat:interior-design','Quote analysis & comparison',9,'A complete fit-out quote should itemise: design/drawings & authority-approval coordination (DM/DDA/Trakhees/DCD); demolition & protection; partitions (gypsum/glazed) with stud gauge and board type; ceilings (gypsum vs metal tile, acoustic rating); flooring (porcelain/vinyl/SPC/carpet, m2 + skirting); MEP — electrical DB/points, HVAC (FCU/ducting/diffusers/BMS), plumbing, fire alarm & sprinkler modifications, low-current (data/CCTV/access); joinery; painting; signage; testing/commissioning; cleaning; AMC/snagging. Differentiators carry units: overall AED/sqft or AED/m2; partition AED/m2; ceiling type & acoustic NRC; flooring grade (PEI rating, mm); HVAC scope (new vs tap-off existing); finishes grade (standard vs premium marble/veneer). Spot a strong quote: drawings included, MEP fully scoped, brand/spec named, approvals & fees stated, realistic timeline (office 6-12 weeks). Underpriced quotes cut MEP (exclude HVAC balancing, fire-system mods, BMS), use thinner partitions, fewer power points, generic finishes, and EXCLUDE authority/permit/DM/DCD/DEWA fees and VAT (5%). Highlight to a customer: is it supply-and-install or supply-only; are approvals/permits included; finishes grade and brand named or ''as per sample''; is MEP a full design or ''allowance/PC sum''; defects-liability period. Common tricks: vague ''as per drawing'' with no spec, provisional sums hiding real cost, sample swapped for cheaper after award.'),
('cat:joinery-kitchens','Authoritative sources & references',4,'Blum (hardware specifications) — https://www.blum.com/
Hettich (hardware specifications) — https://www.hettich.com/
Hafele Middle East — https://www.hafele.ae/
EN 13986 / EN 717-1 formaldehyde & wood-panel standards (overview) — https://arcedior.com/blog/e1-e0-formaldehyde-board-standards
