('cat:false-ceiling','Standards, materials & specifications',8,'Gypsum boards are classified to EN 520 (and DIN 18180) or ASTM C1396, with core types: regular/standard; moisture-resistant (water-repellent, ''green'' board) for bathrooms/kitchens/humid zones; fire-rated Type X (glass-fibre reinforced core) and the higher-performance Type C; acoustic (high-density); impact-resistant; and combined fire+moisture boards. Typical board thicknesses are 9.5, 12.5 and 15mm; a single 12.5/15mm Type X layer each side of studs commonly gives a 1-hour rating, with higher ratings via double layers and the manufacturer''s certified system. Framing uses galvanised steel sections (perimeter channels, CD furring/ceiling sections, and partition studs/tracks 48-100mm) at standard centres (typically 600mm); cavity insulation is rock or glass mineral wool (density and thickness drive acoustics/fire). Performance vocabulary: fire resistance in minutes/EI rating, acoustic Rw (dB), board density, and the tested system reference (not just the board). Jointing uses tape and joint compound; finishes are skim/putty then paint. Leading brands: Knauf, Gyproc (Saint-Gobain), USG Boral. Always match the board type to the area (moisture board in wet zones) and cite the certified fire-rated system, not a board alone.'),
('cat:tiling-marble','Regulations, licensing & approvals',8,'Tiling and marble work is delivered within fit-out or construction and needs the relevant permit by location: Dubai Municipality (DM) Building Permit System for mainland, Dubai Development Authority (DDA) fit-out permit in its free-zone districts, or Trakhees for Nakheel/free-zone areas; the contractor must be DM/DDA-licensed and drawings submitted by a licensed consultant. The Dubai Building Code and DM finishing guidelines apply, including slip resistance in wet and public areas. Dubai Civil Defence (DCD) NOC is relevant where stone/tiling affects fire compartmentation, escape routes or cladding fire performance; obtain NOCs as required. Stone, tiles, adhesives and sealers are subject to ESMA/MoIAT conformity (ECAS) and should carry test/quality documentation; imported natural stone may also need standard customs/quality documentation. For major fit-outs affecting 50%+ of built-up area, Al Sa''fat 2.0 Silver sustainability compliance can apply, which can affect material sourcing and low-VOC sealers/adhesives. Practical workflow: appoint licensed contractor/consultant, prepare finishes/setting-out drawings, obtain any DCD NOC, secure fit-out/building permit (DM/DDA/Trakhees), install to spec with proper sealing, then inspection and completion sign-off. Always confirm the governing authority by the project''s exact location before committing to a programme.'),
('cat:interior-design','Customer intake & scoping questions',9,'1) Property type and location (office/retail/F&B/clinic/villa) and the building/community — is it Dubai Municipality area, a DDA/TECOM free zone, Trakhees/Nakheel, or DIFC? This sets the approval authority. 2) Total area in sqft/m2 and a floor plan or site visit. 3) Is this a bare-shell, grey-shell or existing fit-out being modified? 4) Scope: which works — partitions, ceilings, flooring, MEP, joinery, painting, glazing, signage? 5) Finish/quality level: standard, mid or premium (and any reference images or brand preferences)? 6) MEP needs: extra AC capacity, kitchen extraction, additional power/data, fire-system changes? 7) For F&B/clinics — commercial kitchen, grease trap, medical gas, special ventilation? 8) Do they need design/drawings or are approved drawings ready? 9) Will the contractor handle DM/DDA/DCD/DEWA approvals and pay fees, or client-arranged? 10) Building NOC and landlord conditions/working-hours restrictions? 11) Timeline and any hard handover/opening date. 12) Budget range or AED/sqft target. 13) Access/working hours (mall night-work, restricted lift) and storage. 14) Defects-liability period and any AMC required.'),
('cat:painting','Quote analysis & comparison',9,'A complete painting quote should state, per area/m2: surface preparation (cleaning, scraping, sanding, crack filling, putty/skim, masking and protection); primer/sealer (type and one or more coats — e.g. alkali-resistant or wall sealer); and topcoat system (brand, product line, sheen, and number of coats — typically 2 finish coats). It should separate ceilings, internal walls, external walls, and woodwork/metalwork (which use different primers and enamels). Differentiators with units: paint brand and exact product (Jotun, Dulux/Caparol, National Paints), finish/sheen (matt, silk/eggshell, satin, gloss), number of coats (1 vs 2), VOC level (low/zero-VOC vs standard), and m2 rate. Strong quote: names brand and product line, sheen, primer type, and 2 topcoats; includes prep, putty, protection and making-good; states m2 and any minimum-coverage. Corner-cutting and which corner gets cut: skipping primer/sealer; one topcoat instead of two (looks fine initially, patchy later); thinning paint to stretch coverage; minimal prep (no proper crack filling/putty); cheaper sub-brand swapped after the sample. Tricks to flag: ''paint included'' without brand/product or coat count; supply-only vs supply-and-apply; scaffolding/access for high or external walls excluded; furniture moving/protection and post-work cleaning excluded; warranty period unstated; VAT excluded.'),
