('cat:tiling-marble','Pricing benchmarks & red flags',7,'Indicative UAE prices (verify per project; exclude VAT 5% unless stated). Material supply varies hugely by stone: granite slabs roughly AED 250-650/m2 (entry to premium), polished marble at 20mm roughly from ~AED 185/m2 for common types up to several hundred or thousands for premium Italian/exotic; porcelain marble-effect tiles are cheaper. Marble/stone installation labour commonly roughly AED 35-80/m2 depending on complexity, with stairs, mitred edges, book-matching and large slabs adding cost; sealing, polishing and crystallisation are separate. Price drivers: stone origin/grade, thickness, finish, piece size/slab handling, edge and skirting work, substrate levelling, sealing/polishing, and access for heavy slabs. Red flags: prices far below market usually mean thinner stone (15-16mm vs 18-20mm), lower-grade/filled/B-grade stone or origin substitution, cement mortar or a staining grey adhesive instead of stone-safe C2 adhesive, no penetrating sealer (leading to staining/etching), skipped levelling, and no final polish/crystallisation. Also watch for supply-only disguised as installed, excluded sealing/polishing, excluded skirting/stairs/special cuts, hidden VAT and wastage, and vague ''marble work'' with no stated origin, thickness or finish. Confirm a sealing line item exists for marble/limestone.'),
('cat:interior-design','Standards, materials & specifications',8,'Governing fire standard: UAE Fire and Life Safety Code of Practice (Dubai Civil Defence, 2018 edition with updates) — dictates fire-rated partitions, fire-stopping, emergency lighting, exit/travel-distance, sprinkler and alarm requirements, and surface-spread-of-flame ratings for finishes (Class 0/1 in exit routes). Partitions: gypsum board (12.5mm standard, fire-rated/moisture-resistant variants) on galvanised steel studs; fire-rated partitions specified by hours (e.g. 1-hr, 2-hr). Ceilings: gypsum board or metal/mineral acoustic tiles with NRC ratings. Flooring: porcelain tile (PEI III-IV for commercial), SPC/LVT (wear-layer in mm), broadloom/carpet tile. HVAC commonly DX or chilled-water FCUs with ducting and diffusers; BMS for larger projects. Electrical to DEWA regulations; low-current per IT brief. Joinery boards should be E1 low-formaldehyde (per EN 13986/EN 717-1) and moisture-resistant in wet areas. Sustainability: Dubai''s Al Sa''fat Green Building System (mainland) and Estidama (Abu Dhabi) influence material/VOC specs. Always confirm finishes by named brand/model, RAL/colour, and m2/quantity rather than ''as per sample''.'),
('cat:cctv-security','Closed-case lesson (RFQ-bf2f91d7-55d2-4937-8311-55184205c57e)',60,'Zero supplier responses at cancellation time means no market-rate data was captured for access control (14 units, Ajman); building a benchmark price log even from cancelled RFQs would improve future budget guidance for this category.'),
('cat:cctv-security','Closed-case lesson (RFQ-bf2f91d7-55d2-4937-8311-55184205c57e)',60,'The AED 5,000–15,000 budget range is wide (3x spread) for a defined quantity of 14 units; prompting customers to confirm per-unit budget expectations during RFQ creation would sharpen supplier targeting and reduce premature cancellations caused by misaligned expectations.'),
('cat:waterproofing','Regulations, licensing & approvals',8,'The Dubai Building Code (2021 Edition, official, on dm.gov.ae) is the governing technical document; its verified principle is that a full waterproof tanking system must be provided for all elements exposed to water, and structural elements that cannot be accessed for maintenance are treated specially. Exact clause numbers for wet-area/roof/basement DFTs should be confirmed in the PDF before being quoted as fact. Administrative Resolution No. (37) of 2021 (Dubai Legislation portal) amends the Bylaw on Building Requirements and Specifications and is the legal instrument behind the tanking/waterproofing requirements. Dubai Central Laboratory (DCL) product certification (verified): DCL certifies building materials against DM regulations and adopted standards, is accredited to ISO/IEC 17065 and approved by EIAC (Cert. No. CB-PRD-001), and for products not covered by an adopted standard issues a Technical Approval; the process runs initial factory assessment, product testing, certification licence, then surveillance. Identified building products - including waterproofing membranes - must carry a valid DCL Certificate of Conformity / Conformity Mark before use or sale in Dubai, and consultants routinely demand it. Approved applicator/contractor: there is no single public DM ''approved waterproofing applicator'' registry found; applicator approval in practice flows through DM/Trakhees/DDA contractor licensing plus the membrane manufacturer''s approved-applicator scheme tied to the system warranty (confirm with DM directly). Warranty norms: a 10-year full-system warranty is the de-facto developer-project standard (e.g. Sika systems commonly mandated on Emaar projects); note this is distinct from the Emaar homeowner Defect Liability Period of 1 year (which covers the building envelope preventing water penetration) and from the UAE Civil Code 10-year decennial liability for structural collapse. Fit-out/NOC: any wet-area relocation, partition or MEP change in DM-jurisdiction towers requires a DM fit-out permit; in free zones it routes through DDA/Trakhees fit-out permits and the developer NOC, with wet-zone changes in the strict-control category.'),
('cat:aluminium-glass','Customer intake & scoping questions',9,'1) What is the work: windows, doors, sliding/folding, curtain wall, structural glazing, shopfront, balustrade, skylight, or partitions? 2) Quantities and sizes per opening, or total facade/area in m2? 3) Property type and floor level (villa, apartment, tower, retail, industrial) and is access at height needed (scaffold/crane)? 4) Glazing preference: single or double/IGU, and any U-value/SHGC or acoustic target? 5) Glass type: clear, tinted, reflective, Low-E, tempered, laminated/safety, fire-rated? 6) Frame finish: powder-coat (RAL colour) or anodised, and required microns? 7) Thermal break required (for energy/Al Sa''fat compliance)? 8) Operability: fixed, casement, sliding, tilt-turn; mesh/insect screens? 9) Hardware/lock brand preference and any motorisation? 10) Compliance needs: DCD fire-rated certificate, structural-glazing wind-load calcs, Al Sa''fat green building? 11) Is this supply-only or supply & install, and who removes existing units? 12) Site readiness, timeline/handover date, and working-hours restrictions? 13) Budget range and is VAT to be shown separately? 14) Warranty expectation on glass, frame finish and sealant?'),
('cat:flooring','Quote analysis & comparison',9,'A complete flooring quote (tiles/parquet/epoxy) shows: material make/model and size, substrate prep (screed/self-levelling, moisture barrier), adhesive/underlay type, installation pattern, grouting/coving, skirting/transitions/profiles, sealing/finishing, removal of existing, wastage %, and supply & install vs supply-only. Differentiating spec parameters (with units): TILES - porcelain vs ceramic, size (e.g. 600x600, 600x1200mm), thickness (8-12mm+), PEI abrasion class (1-5), slip rating R9-R13 (DIN 51130)/COF, water absorption (porcelain <0.5%), rectified vs non-rectified; PARQUET/WOOD - engineered vs solid vs laminate, wear/AC rating (laminate AC3-AC5), top-layer/veneer thickness (mm), total thickness, finish; EPOXY - system type (self-levelling/screed), thickness (e.g. 1-3mm, up to 5mm industrial), primer + topcoat coats, anti-slip aggregate, coving. A strong quote names brand, PEI/R/AC ratings, thickness, adhesive class and includes screed/levelling; an underpriced one cuts corners by omitting substrate prep/self-levelling, using a cheaper C1 adhesive instead of C2 (EN 12004), thinner tile/veneer, lower PEI/R rating for the traffic, fewer epoxy coats/microns, and no sealing. Highlight to customers: material and ratings (PEI/R/AC), thickness, adhesive class, screed/levelling inclusion, grout/sealing, and wastage. Common tricks: supply-only quoted like installed, screed/levelling excluded, skirting/profiles excluded, VAT and wastage hidden, off-brand substitution, ''epoxy floor'' with no thickness or coats stated.'),
('cat:tiling-marble','Authoritative sources & references',4,'Dubai Municipality (Building Permits / Dubai Building Code) — https://www.dm.gov.ae/
