ISO 10545 / PEI (porcelain abrasion) — https://www.iso.org/
Al Sa''fat Green Building (low-VOC sealers/materials) — https://www.dm.gov.ae/municipality-business/al-safat-dubai-green-building/'),
('cat:interior-design','Pricing benchmarks & red flags',7,'Indicative only — verify per project. Office fit-out commonly runs roughly AED 250-800 per sqft: budget/back-office around AED 180-300, standard corporate around AED 300-500, premium with luxury materials and smart systems up to AED 800+. Retail indicative: basic AED 350-600/sqft, mid-range AED 600-1,000, luxury AED 1,000-1,800+. F&B and clinics sit higher due to MEP, extraction and DCD requirements. Drivers: area, finishes grade, MEP intensity, bespoke joinery, glazing, approvals complexity, and site access (mall night-work). Add 5% VAT and authority/permit fees on top. Red flags: a quote far below peers (often excludes MEP balancing, fire-system mods, approvals/permit/DM/DCD/DEWA fees, or VAT); ''provisional/PC sums'' covering large scope; vague ''as per drawing/sample'' with no named spec or brand; no design or approval coordination listed; unrealistically short timeline; no defects-liability/snagging period; unlicensed contractor; finishes downgraded after award. Compare like-for-like scope, not just headline AED/sqft.'),
('cat:painting','Standards, materials & specifications',8,'A paint system = surface prep + primer/sealer + topcoats. Interior walls typically use acrylic emulsion (water-based) in matt or silk; woodwork/metal use enamel/gloss or PU; exteriors use weather-, UV- and fungus-resistant acrylic or elastomeric coatings suited to the harsh UAE climate. Primers/sealers: wall sealer, alkali-resistant primer (essential on new/fresh plaster to prevent efflorescence), wood primer and metal/anti-corrosive primer. Coat count: generally 1 primer + 2 finish coats for a sound result. Sheen vocabulary: matt, eggshell/silk, satin, semi-gloss, gloss. VOC: low-VOC and zero-VOC lines exist (e.g. Jotun Majestic with zero-VOC and anti-bacterial options) and are preferred under green-building specs and for children''s rooms, nurseries and healthcare. Major brands in the UAE: Jotun (and its decorative ranges), Dulux/Caparol, National Paints, Asian Paints, and Hempel for protective coatings. Specify by named brand and exact product line, sheen, colour (RAL/brand code), primer type, number of coats and VOC class. Confirm coverage/spreading rate and dry-film expectations rather than vague ''good-quality paint''.'),
('cat:aluminium-glass','Regulations, licensing & approvals',8,'Work must be done by a Dubai Municipality (DM)-licensed contractor; drawings for facade/structural-glazing works are submitted by DM-licensed consultants through DM''s Building Permit System (BPS), which from 2026 runs automated compliance scanning under the Dubai Building Code and Al Sa''fat 2.0 green building framework (Silver is the mandatory baseline for new permits). Free-zone and special-development areas have their own authorities: Dubai Development Authority (DDA) issues fit-out/construction permits and NOCs in its jurisdiction (e.g. media/internet/design districts), and Trakhees (Ports, Customs & Free Zone Corporation/EHS) governs Palm/Nakheel and many free-zone developments. Dubai Civil Defence (DCD) approval/NOC is required for fire-rated glazing, smoke barriers and any work touching life-safety; DCD references the UAE Fire and Life Safety Code of Practice. Before a permit, NOCs are typically needed from DEWA, telecom (du/Etisalat) and, where roads are affected, RTA. Building products such as safety/architectural glass and aluminium systems generally fall under ESMA/MoIAT conformity (Emirates Conformity Assessment Scheme, ECAS) and may require test certificates. Always confirm the exact authority by the project''s location (DM vs DDA vs Trakhees) and obtain completion/inspection sign-off.'),
('cat:flooring','Customer intake & scoping questions',9,'1) Flooring type wanted: porcelain/ceramic tiles, natural stone, engineered/solid wood parquet, laminate, vinyl/SPC, or epoxy/resin? 2) Area in m2 per room and total, with room functions? 3) Property/space type: villa, apartment, office, retail, warehouse, kitchen, bathroom, balcony/external? 4) Traffic level (light residential to heavy commercial/industrial) - drives PEI/AC/epoxy thickness? 5) Wet/slip-critical areas (bathroom, pool deck, kitchen) needing slip rating R10-R13? 6) Tile size, finish (matt/polished), and rectified edges; or wood species/finish; or epoxy colour/finish (matt/gloss/3D)? 7) Current floor: bare slab, old tiles, or screed - removal needed, and is levelling/self-levelling required? 8) Thickness/build-up limits (door clearances, thresholds)? 9) Pattern/layout: straight, diagonal, herringbone, large-format; grout colour? 10) Edges/finishes: skirting, transition profiles, coving (epoxy), sealing? 11) Compliance/standards needed and DM/DDA fit-out approval? 12) Supply & install or supply-only; who supplies material? 13) Timeline, site access, and curing/downtime tolerance (epoxy/screed)? 14) Budget range and VAT shown separately?'),
('cat:interior-design','Authoritative sources & references',4,'Dubai Municipality — https://www.dm.gov.ae/
Dubai Development Authority, Fit-Out Permit — https://dda.gov.ae/en/planning-development/construction/permits-nocs/fit-out-permit
Trakhees (Ports, Customs & Free Zone Corporation) — https://www.trakhees.ae/
DIFC, Submittals & External Works NOC — https://www.difc.com/business/submittals-requests-and-external-works-noc
Dubai Civil Defence — https://www.dcd.gov.ae/
UAE Fire and Life Safety Code of Practice — https://www.dcd.gov.ae/portal/en/preventive-safety/rules-regulations/uae-fire-and-life-safety-code-of-practice.jsp
DEWA — https://www.dewa.gov.ae/
Dubai Al Sa''fat Green Building System (Dubai Municipality) — https://www.dm.gov.ae/
Department of Economy and Tourism (DET) — https://www.dubaided.gov.ae/
Commercial fit-out cost benchmarks (West Gate) — https://westgatedubai.com/commercial-fit%E2%80%91out-costs-in-dubai-office-retail-fb-and-warehouse-benchmarks/
Restaurant fit-out & DCD guide — https://daemuae.com/interior-fitout-approvals/complete-guide-to-restaurant-fit-out-approval/'),
('cat:painting','Pricing benchmarks & red flags',7,'Indicative only — painting is usually priced per m2 of painted surface or as a per-room/per-unit package, and varies with brand, coats, prep and access. Budget interior repaints with a standard emulsion and basic prep sit at the low end; mid-range work with branded Jotun/Dulux, proper primer and two coats is higher; premium specifications (zero-VOC, designer/feature finishes, stucco/texture, external weatherproof systems) command the most. Drivers: area, interior vs exterior, surface condition and prep required, brand and product line, number of coats, sheen, special finishes, and access (scaffolding/cradle for high or facade work). Add 5% VAT and any access/scaffolding cost. Red flags: a quote well below peers usually means skipped primer, only one topcoat, minimal prep, thinned paint, or an unnamed/sub-brand product swapped for the quoted sample; ''paint included'' with no brand, product or coat count; supply-only quoted against a competitor''s supply-and-apply; protection, furniture moving and final cleaning excluded; no warranty stated; access/scaffolding excluded from a high-wall or external job.'),
('_global','Closed-case lesson (RFQ-06245f8a-5a44-45f9-b9ea-1927f4df7a25)',60,'Enforce mandatory-field completeness as a hard gate before a quote reaches the customer: both submissions lacked trade licence reference, BOQ, payment terms, validity, and exclusions — the minimum set needed for a defensible award decision. A submission scoring below a defined threshold (e.g., fewer than 8 of 11 mandatory fields) should be auto-held and the supplier prompted to resubmit before the quote is surfaced.'),
('_global','Closed-case lesson (RFQ-06245f8a-5a44-45f9-b9ea-1927f4df7a25)',60,'Rejected or unverified suppliers (BlueBay approval_status: rejected, verified: false) should be blocked from submitting quotes on live RFQs, or at minimum flagged prominently to the customer before comparison; allowing their quote into the comparison table creates false competition and potential compliance risk.'),
('_global','Closed-case lesson (RFQ-06245f8a-5a44-45f9-b9ea-1927f4df7a25)',60,'For turnkey fit-out RFQs, require an itemised BOQ as a structured form field rather than a free-text or file upload — both suppliers omitted it entirely, making cost comparison and scope alignment impossible and increasing dispute risk post-award.'),
('_global','Closed-case lesson (RFQ-06245f8a-5a44-45f9-b9ea-1927f4df7a25)',60,'Quote validity and payment terms should be captured as structured, mandatory date/select fields at submission; both quotes left these blank, meaning any award would be made against terms that are legally unenforceable and commercially ambiguous.'),
('marketing','whatsapp_lifecycle',84,'WhatsApp is the UAE''s primary business channel and QuotesDone''s natural conversion layer — buyers message rather than fill forms. Send promotional WhatsApp/SMS only to contacts who explicitly opted in; use an authorized WhatsApp BSP and pre-approve marketing templates. Promotional SMS: send only 07:00-21:00 GST, use the ''AD-'' sender prefix, and include a free opt-out. Favor utility/service messages (RFQ updates, quote-received alerts) — they are cheaper and fit the 24h customer-initiated window; marketing templates are billed per message. Use Click-to-WhatsApp ads to cut cost-per-lead versus forms. For email, keep SPF/DKIM/DMARC valid or Etisalat/du filters will block you. Verify current TDRA timing and sender-ID rules before any blast.'),
('cat:false-ceiling','Quote analysis & comparison',9,'A complete false-ceiling/gypsum-partition quote shows, per system: board type and brand (Knauf/Gyproc/USG), board thickness (commonly 9.5/12.5/15mm), number of layers per face, framing (galvanised steel section gauge/size, e.g. CD/UD perimeter, stud/track 50/70/100mm and centres 600mm), insulation (rock/glass wool, density kg/m3, thickness), fixings, jointing/taping, two-coat skim/finish, access panels, and supply & install vs supply-only. Differentiating spec parameters (with units): board type (standard/regular, moisture-resistant ''green'', fire-rated Type X, acoustic, impact, or combined fire+moisture), fire rating in minutes (e.g. 60/90/120) and the tested system that achieves it, board thickness (mm), partition stud size and gauge, insulation density and thickness, and acoustic Rw (dB). A strong quote names board type/standard (EN 520/ASTM C1396), fire-rating with the certified system, stud gauge and insulation; an underpriced one cuts corners with thinner board (9.5 where 12.5mm specified), single-layer where double is needed for fire rating, lower steel gauge/wider stud centres, regular board in wet areas (no moisture board), no insulation, and skim-only finish. Highlight to customers: fire rating + certified system, board type per area (moisture in wet zones), stud gauge/centres, insulation, and finish level. Common tricks: regular board substituted for fire/moisture rated, single-layer claiming a 2-layer rating, VAT/wastage excluded, access panels and painting excluded, vague ''gypsum ceiling'' with no board type.'),
('cat:flooring','Authoritative sources & references',4,'Dubai Municipality (Building Permits / Dubai Building Code) — https://www.dm.gov.ae/
Dubai Development Authority (Fit-Out Permit) — https://dda.gov.ae/en/planning-development/construction/permits-nocs/fit-out-permit
Trakhees (PCFC / EHS) — https://www.trakhees.ae/
Dubai Civil Defence (NOC where applicable) — https://www.dcd.gov.ae/
ESMA / MoIAT (ECAS conformity) — https://www.moiat.gov.ae/
EN 12004 (tile adhesives classification) — https://standards.cencenelec.eu/
Laticrete EN 12004 classification (TDS 137EN) — https://cdn-global.laticrete.com/-/media/project/laticrete-international/shared/files/support-and-downloads/technical-datasheets/tds137en.ashx
Mapei UAE (adhesives/grouts/resin) — https://www.mapei.com/ae/en/
Sika UAE (Sikafloor epoxy) — https://gcc.sika.com/
DIN 51130 (R-rating slip resistance) — https://www.din.de/
PEI / ISO 10545 abrasion (porcelain) — https://www.iso.org/
Al Sa''fat Green Building (VOC/materials) — https://www.dm.gov.ae/municipality-business/al-safat-dubai-green-building/'),
('cat:joinery-kitchens','Standards, materials & specifications',8,'Boards: MDF, particleboard (chipboard), plywood and HPL — carcasses commonly 18mm; wet/humid areas should use moisture-resistant (MR) board (often green-tinted). Formaldehyde emission class is key: E1 allows up to ~0.1 ppm and is verified per EN 13986/EN 717-1; many UAE hospitality and commercial specs require E1 (or stricter E0/CARB) minimum, with test reports. Finishes vocabulary: melamine (low cost), HPL/high-pressure laminate (durable), PVC/thermofoil wrap, acrylic (high gloss), PU/lacquer (sprayed, seamless), and natural wood veneer (oak, walnut, ash). Edge-banding: PVC, or premium PUR/laser-edge for seamless waterproof joints. Hardware benchmarks: Blum (Austria) and Hettich (Germany) for hinges, runners (e.g. Blum Tandembox/Legrabox), soft-close and lift systems; Grass, Hafele also common; budget brands (Hi-Tech, M5) cost less. Worktops: post-formed laminate, engineered quartz (e.g. Caesarstone, Silestone), granite, or solid surface (Corian/acrylic) — confirm thickness (20/30mm) and edge profile. Specify by named brand/model, board grade + E1, finish per element, and edge-banding type rather than generic descriptions.'),
('cat:signage','Regulations, licensing & approvals',8,'Signage approval depends on the sign''s location and visibility. Dubai Municipality approves most building/shopfront signs on private facades, including those visible from public roads, and requires a permit application with: trade licence copy, site plan, facade photo, a scaled sign drawing with dimensions, a mock-up/perspective, and a landlord NOC — undated NOCs or non-scaled drawings cause rejection and restart (commonly 5-10 working days). Illuminated signs need DEWA electrical approval, and large or elevated signs require a qualified engineer''s structural plans, material specs, electrical details and wind-load calculations. Signs adjacent to or visible from RTA-managed roads (billboards, hoardings, road-visible advertising) require an RTA signage NOC/permit (which can take up to ~20 working days). Free-zone locations use their own authority — Dubai Development Authority (DDA/TECOM) advertising permit for Media/Internet City etc., or Trakhees for Jebel Ali/Palm/Nakheel areas; DIFC for its district. The signage text/name must match the licensed trade name/activity. Temporary outdoor advertising and digital/LED screens have separate RTA/DM permit routes. Permits, DEWA fees and structural certification are real, often-overlooked costs that should be in the quote.'),
('cat:cleaning','Customer intake & scoping questions',9,'1) What type of cleaning: general/maintenance, deep, move-in/move-out, post-construction, facade/external, carpet/upholstery, or disinfection? 2) Property type and size: studio/1/2/3BR apartment, townhouse, villa (number of bedrooms/bathrooms), office, retail, warehouse, plus approximate sqft? 3) Is it furnished/occupied or empty? 4) Frequency: one-off, or recurring (daily/weekly/bi-weekly/monthly) and contract duration? 5) Preferred number of cleaners and hours, or do you want us to recommend based on area? 6) Should chemicals, equipment and consumables be supplied by us or available on-site? 7) Any specific areas/tasks: inside cabinets, oven/fridge, windows inside and out, balconies, AC vents, grout, carpets, mattresses? 8) For high/external work: building height/floors, access method available (rope access, cradle/BMU, scaffold), and is a building NOC obtainable? 9) Do you require a supervisor on-site and daily reporting? 10) Any compliance needs: SIRA-related, insurance certificate, health cards, female staff preference? 11) Preferred timing: working hours, after-hours, weekends, or fixed completion date? 12) Site access, parking, water and power availability? 13) Budget range or target price per visit/month? 14) Who signs off completion and how is quality measured (checklist/inspection)?'),
('_global','RFQ completeness rule',900,'Do not finalise or route an RFQ until it reaches a completeness score of 70+. An RFQ needs: category, emirate/location, a specific scope description (not one vague line), scope type (supply-only vs turnkey), an indicative budget or range, and at least one supporting detail (trades list, a drawing/photo, or a BOQ). If any are missing, ask focused clarifying questions for the missing items before proceeding, guided by the category''s engineered qualification checklist.'),
('cat:false-ceiling','Pricing benchmarks & red flags',7,'Indicative UAE prices (verify per project; exclude VAT 5% unless stated): plain gypsum/plasterboard false ceiling roughly AED 45-90/m2 supply & install for standard board; fire-rated or double-layer systems higher; moisture-resistant board adds cost; suspended grid tile ceilings vary by tile. Gypsum partitions (single board each face, insulated) commonly run roughly AED 90-180/m2 depending on board type, layers, stud gauge and insulation; fire-rated double-layer partitions cost more. Price drivers: board type (fire/moisture/acoustic vs regular), number of layers, steel section gauge and stud centres, insulation density/thickness, ceiling complexity (coves, bulkheads, curves), height/access, and finish level (board-only vs skim+paint). Red flags: price well below market usually means thinner board (9.5 vs 12.5mm), single layer claiming a multi-layer fire rating, lighter steel gauge or wider stud centres, regular board used in wet areas, no cavity insulation where acoustics/fire are specified, and a stated fire rating with no certified system reference. Also watch for excluded access panels, excluded painting, omitted VAT and wastage, and vague ''gypsum works'' scope with no board type, thickness or rating.'),
('cat:tiling-marble','Standards, materials & specifications',8,'Natural stone: marble (calcareous, polishable, prone to staining/etching - needs sealing), granite (hard, durable, suitable for counters/heavy floors), limestone/travertine (porous, sealing critical), onyx (decorative). Typical thicknesses: slabs 18-20mm, countertops 20mm, floor slabs up to 30mm, wall tiles thinner. Finishes: polished (reflective), honed (matt, less slip), leathered/brushed, flamed (granite, slip-resistant external). Quality vocabulary: origin/grade, porosity/water absorption, flexural strength, and whether stone is filled/resin-treated. Fixing: cement mortar/slurry for thick beds, or notched-trowel adhesive to EN 12004 (C2 improved, often a non-staining white or stone-specific adhesive to avoid discolouration), grouts to EN 13888; large/heavy slabs may need anchored/mechanical fixing on facades. Sealing uses penetrating impregnators; marble is finished by polishing and crystallisation. For ceramic/porcelain tiling, the same PEI (abrasion 1-5), DIN 51130 R-rating (slip) and water-absorption metrics apply. Reputable supply: Italian/Spanish/Turkish marble houses and UAE stone suppliers; adhesives/sealers by Mapei, Sika, Weber, Laticrete, Sodamco. Match stone thickness to application, use stone-safe adhesive, and always specify sealing for marble/limestone.'),
('cat:interior-design','Regulations, licensing & approvals',8,'The approving authority depends on jurisdiction. Most mainland Dubai falls under Dubai Municipality (DM): a fit-out permit is issued after submission of architectural and MEP drawings, building-owner NOC, and contractor/consultant trade licences; technical review and comments are typical, then permit issuance (commonly 3-10 working days), site inspections, and a completion/clearance certificate. Free-zone and master-developer areas use their own authority: Dubai Development Authority (DDA/TECOM) for Media/Internet/Knowledge City etc.; Trakhees (Ports, Customs & Free Zone Corp / Nakheel) for Jebel Ali, Palm, Discovery Gardens; DIFC for its district. Fire and life safety is governed by Dubai Civil Defence (DCD): a DCD NOC against the UAE Fire and Life Safety Code of Practice is required, especially for F&B, clinics and assembly occupancies (fire alarm linked to Hassantuk, emergency lighting, sprinkler/extinguisher coverage, kitchen wet-chemical suppression). DEWA approval is needed for electrical/load and water connection works. Contractors and consultants must hold valid DET/free-zone trade licences and registered engineers. Change-of-use requires a separate permit. DET will not issue/renew a trade licence without the DCD completion certificate linked to the premises.'),
('cat:painting','Customer intake & scoping questions',9,'1) What is being painted — full villa/apartment, single rooms, office, exterior facade, or specific elements (ceilings, walls, woodwork, metal)? 2) Area in m2 or number/size of rooms, and ceiling height (affects access). 3) Interior, exterior, or both — and is it new/bare plaster, previously painted, or requiring repair? 4) Surface condition: cracks, damp/mould, peeling, or old wallpaper to remove? 5) Brand and quality preference: Jotun, Dulux/Caparol, National Paints, or budget — and any low/zero-VOC requirement (kids'' rooms, clinics)? 6) Finish/sheen: matt, silk/eggshell, satin or gloss, and colour scheme (RAL/colour codes)? 7) Number of coats expected and whether primer/sealer is needed? 8) Any special finishes — texture, stucco, feature walls, waterproof coating, anti-fungal/anti-bacterial? 9) Furniture present — will they clear it or does the painter protect/move it? 10) Access for high or external work (scaffolding, cradle, ladders)? 11) Property type and any building/community NOC or working-hours rules? 12) Timeline and whether the space is occupied. 13) Budget range. 14) Warranty expected and is post-work cleaning included?'),
('cat:cleaning','Authoritative sources & references',4,'Dubai Municipality — Technical Guidelines List — https://www.dm.gov.ae/municipality-business/technical-guidelines-list/
Dubai Municipality (main portal) — https://www.dm.gov.ae/
Dubai Department of Economy and Tourism (business licensing) — https://www.det.gov.ae/
Dubai Civil Defence (fire & work-at-height safety) — https://www.dcd.gov.ae/
Security Industry Regulatory Agency (SIRA) — https://www.sira.gov.ae/
IRATA International (rope access work-at-height standards) — https://irata.org/
IPAF (powered access / MEWP) — https://www.ipaf.org/
PASMA (mobile access towers) — https://pasma.co.uk/
Dubai Green Building Regulations & Specifications (DM) — https://www.dm.gov.ae/wp-content/uploads/2020/11/Green-Buildings-1.pdf
ServiceMarket — Deep cleaning cost guide Dubai (indicative pricing) — https://servicemarket.com/en/blog/deep-cleaning-cost-price-dubai
Libra Group — Building cleaning rope access & safety rules UAE — https://libragroupuae.com/building-cleaning-services-in-uae-expert-guide-to-rope-access-safety-rules/'),
('_global','Retrieve before answering',980,'Before answering any technical, regulatory, pricing, material or procurement question, FIRST retrieve from the internal knowledge platform (call kb_query / kb_retrieve, and consult the category checklist, regulation library, materials and standards). Base the answer on what you retrieve and cite the source (e.g. the regulation code, standard, or checklist item). If retrieval returns nothing relevant, say so and ask a clarifying question or escalate — never fill the gap with an unsourced guess.'),
('marketing','ramadan_timing',82,'Plan the UAE calendar ahead: Ramadan (verify exact dates each year), Eid Al Fitr, National Day (Dec 2), and national themes. Sourcing intent spikes in Ramadan, so start planning roughly 6 weeks out. Shift tone to community, family, reflection, and charity; drop hard-sell. Acquire buyers and suppliers in the two weeks BEFORE Ramadan — they show higher lifetime value than those acquired mid-month. Peak engagement moves to post-Iftar and Suhoor (roughly 9pm-3am), so schedule content then — but promotional SMS must still stop by 9pm GST. Avoid food/drink imagery during daylight fasting hours. Use Ramadan to nurture intent, then pivot to conversion and remarketing as Eid nears.'),
('cat:flooring','Pricing benchmarks & red flags',7,'Indicative UAE prices (verify per project; exclude VAT 5% unless stated). Material supply varies widely; installation labour and prep are the comparable lines. Tile installation labour often roughly AED 25-60/m2 (large-format/stone higher), plus material and screed/adhesive. Epoxy flooring commonly roughly AED 35-150/m2 (thin coatings low end; self-levelling from ~AED 50; heavy-duty 3-5mm industrial AED 100-150+). Laminate installation roughly AED 20-40/m2 labour, supply+install mid-range ~AED 80-120/m2, premium AED 120-200/m2. Engineered/parquet wood roughly AED 100-300/m2 supply (premium solid/herringbone higher). Price drivers: material grade and ratings (PEI/R/AC), tile size (large-format costs more to lay), substrate condition and self-levelling, pattern complexity (herringbone/diagonal), epoxy thickness and number of coats, removal of existing, and wastage. Red flags: prices far below market usually mean omitted screed/self-levelling, cheaper C1 adhesive instead of C2, thinner tile or veneer, lower PEI/R for the traffic, fewer epoxy coats/under-spec thickness, no sealing, and off-brand substitution. Also watch for supply-only disguised as installed, excluded skirting/transition profiles, hidden VAT and wastage %, no slip rating in wet areas, and ''epoxy floor'' quoted with no thickness or coat count.'),
('cat:joinery-kitchens','Regulations, licensing & approvals',8,'Joinery and cabinet makers operate under a DET (mainland) or free-zone trade licence covering carpentry/joinery/furniture manufacturing, and many run a workshop in industrial zones (e.g. Al Quoz, Al Qusais, Ras Al Khor) under Dubai Municipality and Civil Defence workshop requirements. Standalone furniture supply usually needs no building permit, but joinery delivered as part of a fit-out falls under the project''s Dubai Municipality / DDA / Trakhees fit-out permit and, where fire-rated or in F&B/assembly spaces, the Dubai Civil Defence (DCD) NOC and UAE Fire and Life Safety Code of Practice — finishes in escape routes and certain occupancies must meet surface-spread-of-flame ratings. Plumbing/electrical connections for kitchens (sinks, hobs, extraction, lighting) tie into DEWA-regulated works. Imported boards and panels face customs documentation; E1/formaldehyde and quality claims may require test reports at import clearance or client handover. For building entry, site work and use of common areas, the building owner/community NOC and working-hours rules apply. There is no single dedicated ''joinery permit'' — compliance flows through the host fit-out approval and the workshop''s own municipal/Civil Defence licensing.'),
('cat:signage','Customer intake & scoping questions',9,'1) Sign type: shopfront fascia, 3D built-up letters, lightbox, pylon/totem, wall logo, directional, vehicle, or building/facade sign? 2) Dimensions — overall size and letter height (and is it visible from a public road)? 3) Location and jurisdiction: Dubai Municipality area, a DDA/TECOM free zone, Trakhees, or adjacent to an RTA road (which sets the approval path)? 4) Illuminated or non-illuminated — and if lit, front-lit, halo/back-lit, or edge-lit? 5) Material preference: acrylic, ACP, stainless steel, brushed/mirror finish — and any fire-rated ACP requirement for a facade? 6) Brand artwork, exact colours (Pantone/RAL) and a vector logo file? 7) Mounting surface and height (glass, ACP cladding, masonry) and access for installation (cherry picker, scaffolding)? 8) Does the trade licence name/activity match the signage text (required for approval)? 9) Will the supplier handle DM / DEWA / RTA / free-zone approvals and pay the fees, or client-arranged? 10) Power supply availability near the sign location? 11) Landlord/building NOC and any community sign guidelines? 12) Timeline (note approvals can take days to weeks). 13) Budget range. 14) Warranty on LEDs/structure and any maintenance contract?'),
('cat:cleaning','Quote analysis & comparison',9,'A complete cleaning quote itemises: scope (general/deep/post-construction/facade/carpet/sofa/disinfection), area in sqft or unit count (studio/1BR/villa), frequency (one-off, daily, weekly, per visit), manpower headcount and man-hours per visit, supervisor presence, consumables (chemicals, microfibre, pads, machinery: scrubber/vacuum/steam), access method for high work (rope access/IRATA, suspended cradle/BMU, ladder, scaffold), transport/mobilisation, VAT 5%, and any minimum booking (often 2-3 hrs). For facade/high-rise: third-party cradle certification, work-at-height permit, building NOC, supervisor-to-rope-tech ratio. SPOT A STRONG QUOTE: stated man-hours and headcount (not just a lump price), named chemicals/equipment, method statement and RAMS for height work, insurance cover. UNDERPRICED/CORNER-CUTTING signals and WHICH corner gets cut: too few cleaners or hours for the area; deep clean priced like a regular clean (no scrubbing/grout/inside-cabinet work); rope access quoted without IRATA-certified techs or third-party cradle cert; cheap diluted/uncertified chemicals; VAT hidden, then added. HIGHLIGHT TO CUSTOMER comparing 2-3 quotes: hours x headcount vs area, what each deep clean actually includes (windows inside/out, AC vents, grout, appliances), consumables included vs charged extra, access method and its certification, minimum-hours traps. COMMON TRICKS: vague ''full clean'' without checklist, extras (windows, balcony, fridge) billed separately, no method statement, no insurance, day-labour without supervisor.'),
('_global','AI governance gate',990,'Before emitting a consequential answer or taking an action, it must pass the governance gate: (1) grounded in retrieved knowledge with a cited source, (2) confidence at or above 0.6, (3) authorized for your role (RBAC/ABAC), (4) not tripping an escalation trigger (financial/legal/fraud/sensitive). If any check fails: revise and re-ground, ask a clarifying question, or escalate to the Secretary — never emit an ungrounded, low-confidence, or unauthorized action.'),
('marketing','cro_landing_uae',82,'Trust is the UAE''s conversion currency — put local proof above the fold: a trade-licence or ''Licensed by DED'' badge, a UAE landline (+971), a physical UAE address, and verified supplier counts. Make WhatsApp the primary CTA and track WhatsApp-button clicks as a core conversion — often more reliable than form fills here. Cut form friction: few fields, unchecked consent boxes, a clear privacy link. For an anonymous marketplace, reassure buyers that supplier identity stays hidden until they choose to reveal and pay. Audit ad-to-page message match first, then technical speed (<3s), credibility, offer clarity, and form friction — never buy more traffic to fix a weak page. A/B test the hook before anything else.'),
('marketing','seo_uae_local',80,'Optimize for buyer sourcing intent: target transactional queries like ''get quotes for X in Dubai'', ''suppliers for Y UAE'', ''RFQ [category]'', with separate keyword maps for buyer-side and supplier-side searches. Google dominates UAE search and the top result earns most clicks, so aim for position one on core sourcing terms. Build E-E-A-T with real UAE trust signals: trade licence, physical UAE address, local landline. Complete a Google Business Profile (no P.O. boxes). Add LocalBusiness schema and, for any bilingual pages, correct hreflang (ar-ae/en-ae). A large share of UAE searches are Arabic and Hindi/Urdu/Tagalog volume is significant — keep this in mind for future localization even while posting English. Track AI-search referrals as their own channel.'),
('cat:painting','Regulations, licensing & approvals',8,'Painting contractors operate under a DET (mainland) or relevant free-zone trade licence covering painting/decoration contracting. Standalone interior repainting of an occupied unit generally needs no building permit, but it must respect the building owner/community NOC, working-hours and protection rules (especially in apartment towers and managed communities). When painting is part of a larger fit-out or renovation, it falls under the project''s Dubai Municipality / DDA / Trakhees fit-out permit and any Dubai Civil Defence (DCD) requirements — notably surface-spread-of-flame/Class 0 ratings of coatings in escape routes and certain occupancies under the UAE Fire and Life Safety Code of Practice. External facade painting, scaffolding or rope-access work triggers additional safety requirements and often building-management and Dubai Municipality permissions, plus height-work safety rules. Sustainability programmes — Dubai Municipality''s Al Sa''fat Green Building System (mainland) and Abu Dhabi''s Estidama Pearl Rating — encourage or require low-VOC paints in new and refurbished buildings. Workers must comply with occupational health and safety rules. There is no single dedicated ''paint permit''; compliance flows through the trade licence, building NOC, and any host fit-out/DCD approval.'),
('cat:facilities-management','Quote analysis & comparison',9,'A complete FM quote separates HARD services (MEP: HVAC/chillers/AHUs, electrical/DB/LV, plumbing & drainage, fire & life-safety, BMS, lifts, generators, civil/building fabric) from SOFT services (cleaning, security/guarding, pest control, landscaping, waste, pool). It must list: an asset register/count (number of AC units, pumps, panels, lifts, fire systems) the PPM is priced against; PPM frequency per asset (monthly/quarterly/bi-annual/annual); reactive/breakdown cover with tiered SLA RESPONSE and RESOLUTION times by priority (e.g. critical <30 min response); manning — resident technician headcount and trades vs on-call/mobile crew, shift coverage; consumables/spares included vs excluded (and any spares cap); helpdesk/CAFM reporting; AMC duration and escalation. STRONG QUOTE: named scope with asset counts, PPM schedule, SLA matrix, KPI targets (PPM completion >98%), resident vs mobile manning, clear in/out-of-scope and spares threshold. UNDERPRICED/CORNER-CUTTING and WHICH corner: comprehensive AMC priced like a non-comprehensive (spares excluded, billed later); too few PPM visits or fewer resident staff than the asset load needs; mobile-only cover sold as on-site; vague ''as required'' SLAs with no time commitment; specialist sub-systems (lifts, fire, BMS) silently excluded. HIGHLIGHT TO CUSTOMER: comprehensive vs non-comprehensive (spares in or out), PPM visit count, SLA response/resolution times, resident headcount and hours, what''s excluded (lifts/fire/specialist), KPI/penalty regime. TRICKS: lump-sum with no asset register, no out-of-hours/emergency cover, call-out and spares as hidden extras, VAT omitted.'),
('marketing','paid_search_uae',80,'Structure Google Ads around buyer intent: bid on high-intent sourcing terms (''request a quote'', ''find suppliers UAE''), match location intent to the right emirate, and add negatives to filter out job-seekers and DIY queries. Run separate Arabic and English campaigns to avoid mixed-signal Quality Scores, and write natural idiomatic copy — never machine-translated. Quality Score hinges on message match, so send each ad to a landing page that mirrors its promise. Ensure landing pages carry a PDPL-compliant cookie banner and privacy policy. Regulated categories (finance, healthcare, real estate) face extra Google verification and local permits — verify before running them. Schedule for late-night Ramadan activity. Benchmark CPC/CTR but verify current figures before committing budget.'),
('marketing','b2b_linkedin_uae',80,'LinkedIn drives most UAE B2B leads and fits QuotesDone''s procurement/supplier audience — but the final conversion layer migrates to WhatsApp, so bridge the two. Lead with trust and resonance, not reach: local UAE case studies, real buyer/supplier testimonials, GCC-specific proof — buyers distrust generic global messaging. Target by job title and free zone (DIFC, ADGM, DMCC, JLT), and named accounts for ABM. Use LinkedIn Lead Gen Forms with a PDPL-compliant privacy link and opt-in. Executive thought-leadership with promotional intent may need an advertiser permit — verify. Respect etiquette: correct titles (H.E., Eng., Dr.), and avoid Friday-prayer and fasting-hour send windows. Short-form video is the top-performing format.'),
('marketing','analytics_attribution',78,'Enforce UTM discipline: one documented taxonomy (source_medium_campaign_content_term), lowercase, Latin characters only even for Arabic campaigns, held in a single master sheet so ''facebook'' never splits from ''FB''. Set GA4 to AED currency and Gulf Standard Time so revenue and peak-hour (late-evening) data stay accurate. Use server-side GTM to redact PII before it leaves for US-based servers and to recover signal lost to ad blockers and iOS privacy. Prefer data-driven attribution over last-click. Measure the real funnel to qualified outcomes — RFQs posted, quotes submitted, supplier reveals/paid unlocks — not vanity reach. Show a cookie banner with a genuine Reject All for PDPL. Every report must state what decision it changes.'),
('marketing','arabic_localization',76,'QuotesDone posts English-only for now, but build every asset Arabic-ready. A large share of UAE searches are Arabic and localized content can materially outperform English, so English-only leaves demand on the table. When Arabic launches, transcreate — never machine-translate: use Gulf/Khaleeji dialect for local buyers and Modern Standard Arabic for formal or government-facing copy; Levantine or North African dialects read as foreign. Have a native speaker review register and cultural fit, not just grammar. Design for RTL now — mirrored layout, right-aligned forms, swapped next/prev nav — so the retrofit is cheap. Natural Arabic-English code-switching suits younger cosmopolitan users. Keep UTMs and technical tags in Latin characters regardless of content language.'),
('marketing','influencer_uae',74,'Before engaging any UAE creator, verify they hold both a trade/freelance licence and a valid UAE Media Council Advertiser Permit — it covers paid, gifted, and barter promotion. Require a contractual warranty of both plus a morality clause and defined usage rights. All sponsored content must be disclosed prominently (#ad, #sponsored, Paid Partnership), never buried in ''see more''. Vet on audience fit, genuine UAE follower share, engagement quality, and brand safety — not follower count; micro and nano creators suit a niche B2B sourcing marketplace better than mega names. Content must respect Islamic values and national symbols. Permit fees, enforcement, and rates are volatile — verify current UAE Media Council requirements before contracting.'),
('cat:signage','Quote analysis & comparison',9,'A complete signage quote should itemise: design/artwork & approval drawings; the sign type and substrate (acrylic, ACP/aluminium composite panel, stainless steel, foam/PVC, flex/vinyl); fabrication method (CNC-cut, laser, routing); illumination (front-lit, back-lit/halo, edge-lit, LED module type, transformer/driver, dimming); structure/mounting (frame, raceway, fixings, wind-load engineering for large/high signs); electrical connection; transportation; installation (with access equipment); and crucially the authority approval fees and permits. Differentiators with units: material and thickness (e.g. 3-5mm acrylic, 3-4mm ACP, mm of steel face); letter height and depth; illuminated vs non-illuminated; LED brand/quality and IP rating; fire-rated ACP (A2/B grade) for facades. Strong quote: names material + thickness, illumination type and LED spec, mounting/structure detail, wind-load certification for large signs, and lists DM/DEWA/RTA approval fees separately. Corner-cutting: thin acrylic/ACP that warps in heat; low-grade non-IP-rated LEDs that fail; non-fire-rated ACP; weak fixings/no wind-load calc; cheaper face material swapped after the mock-up. Tricks to flag: approval/permit fees (Dubai Municipality, DEWA, and RTA for road-visible signs), structural engineering certification, and installation labour ''rarely included in the initial fabrication quote'' — confirm they are in scope; supply-only vs supply-and-install; VAT excluded; vague ''LED sign'' with no IP/brand.'),
('marketing','pr_crisis_uae',72,'Protect QuotesDone''s reputation with government and cultural sensitivity. Never publish rumors, unverified claims, or anything harming UAE national unity, religion, or leadership — the Cybercrimes Law treats fake news seriously. Address UAE leaders with correct full titles and protocol; never abbreviate leadership names. Avoid patriotic or political themes unless clearly authorized. In a crisis (a supplier scam, a data incident, a bad-actor buyer), acknowledge awareness fast, avoid speculation, confirm facts are being verified, give a next-update time, and log every decision and evidence source. Align positive messaging with national agendas only where genuine. Measure sentiment and outcomes, not vanity or advertising-value-equivalency metrics.'),
('cat:signage','Pricing benchmarks & red flags',7,'Indicative only — signage is generally priced per square metre of sign area or per piece, and scales with size, material, illumination and finish. Rough market references: non-illuminated acrylic small panels around AED 300-800; backlit acrylic for shopfronts roughly AED 800-2,500; illuminated 3D letters around AED 1,500-8,000 for standard shopfront runs, rising to AED 15,000-40,000+ for large tower/hotel installations; lightboxes roughly AED 800-4,500 for standard sizes; large custom/outdoor signage from a few thousand to AED 100,000+. Larger signs are more cost-efficient per m2 because approval and install labour do not scale proportionally. Crucially, Dubai Municipality approval, DEWA electrical permits, structural engineering certification and installation labour can add roughly AED 1,000-5,000 and are ''rarely included in the initial fabrication quote'' — confirm whether they are in scope. Add 5% VAT. Red flags: a quote well below peers often means thin acrylic/ACP that warps, non-IP-rated/low-grade LEDs that fail in heat, non-fire-rated ACP on a facade, or weak fixings with no wind-load calc; permit/DEWA/RTA fees and installation excluded; supply-only vs supply-and-install; face material downgraded after the mock-up; no LED warranty.'),
('cat:cleaning','Standards, materials & specifications',8,'Core scope vocabulary: general/maintenance clean (dusting, mopping, sanitising surfaces, bathrooms, kitchen wipe-down); deep clean (descaling, grout, inside appliances, cabinet interiors, AC grilles, full window detail); post-construction clean (debris, paint/cement removal, fine-dust passes). Equipment: rotary scrubber-driers, single-disc machines, wet/dry vacuums, steam cleaners, carpet extractors, telescopic poles, water-fed pole systems. Access for height work: rope access governed by IRATA International work-at-height practice (Level 1-3 technicians, supervisor on every job); IPAF certification for MEWPs/cherry pickers; PASMA for mobile scaffold towers; suspended cradles/BMUs requiring valid third-party (e.g., LEEA-type) inspection certificates. Personnel safety competencies commonly cited: NEBOSH/IOSH for supervisors, Work-at-Height training, harnesses and fall-arrest PPE. Chemicals should be DM-registered/approved, with Material Safety Data Sheets (MSDS) available; colour-coded microfibre and equipment to prevent cross-contamination (red/blue/green/yellow zoning) is best practice in commercial and food-area cleaning. Disinfection should specify the product, contact/dwell time and target organisms. A professional method statement (RAMS) and a defined cleaning checklist/scope-of-work per area are the documents that make quotes comparable and define quality.'),
('marketing','library_reference',70,'A deep UAE marketing knowledge library (regulation/PDPL, SEO, paid search, social, influencer, analytics, WhatsApp/email/SMS lifecycle, B2B/LinkedIn, PR/crisis) is stored as ~317 searchable chunks and retrieved on demand via fn_marketing_library_search(query, limit). marketing_draft auto-injects the top matches for each task. When a task needs depth beyond the always-on rows, rely on that retrieved reference rather than guessing. Treat any legal, regulated-sector, or paid-media specific as verify-before-acting, not settled fact.'),
('cat:cleaning','Regulations, licensing & approvals',8,'A cleaning company must hold a Dubai Department of Economy and Tourism (DET, formerly DED) trade licence for the relevant cleaning activity, and operate under Dubai Municipality (DM) public-health and waste regulations. Where cleaning includes disinfection/pest-adjacent fogging or sanitisation chemicals, DM Public Health & Safety oversight and registered chemicals apply. For high-rise facade and window cleaning, the key approvals are: work-at-height compliance with Dubai Civil Defence and DM safety rules; building management NOC; third-party certification of suspended access equipment (cradle/BMU) before use; and IRATA-certified personnel for rope access (Dubai practice restricts rope work to IRATA-certified technicians). Site security clearance via SIRA may be required where guarding or controlled premises are involved, and many large facilities require staff to hold valid health cards/medical fitness certificates and to be on the company''s visa sponsorship. Waste arising from cleaning (especially construction or hazardous waste) must be disposed of through DM-approved channels. NOC workflow for facade work typically: secure building/owner-association NOC, prepare RAMS/method statement, obtain work-at-height permit, verify equipment and operator certification, then mobilise. Always confirm the contractor''s trade-licence activity matches the actual scope (e.g., a general cleaning licence may not cover external rope-access works).'),
('_global','company:self_knowledge',95,'COMPANY SELF-KNOWLEDGE (authoritative org facts — you may answer any question about the company from this):
Vision: Become the smartest marketplace engine in the world.
Totals: 10 departments, 12 brain agents, 45 category sales agents, 5 wired tools, 1 open objectives, 1 open tasks.
Departments (lead · brain agents · north star):
- Analytics & Intelligence [analytics]: lead —, 0 agent(s), north star: insight-to-action latency
- Customer Success [customer_success]: lead followup, 1 agent(s) (followup), north star: reveal conversion / time-to-first-quote
- Executive Office (Chief of Staff) [exec]: lead brief, 1 agent(s) (brief), north star: objectives completed without founder touch
- Finance [finance]: lead cfo, 3 agent(s) (bookkeeper, cfo, collections), north star: cash position / collection rate
- IT & Engineering [it]: lead it, 2 agent(s) (it, security), north star: uptime x change-safety
- Knowledge Management [knowledge]: lead docs, 1 agent(s) (docs), north star: coverage % / gap-close time
- Legal & Compliance [legal]: lead legal, 1 agent(s) (legal), north star: PDPL SLA / open legal risk
- Marketing [marketing]: lead marketing, 1 agent(s) (marketing), north star: cost per qualified inquiry
- Procurement (Marketplace Core) [procurement]: lead —, 45 agent(s), north star: quotes per RFQ / time-to-first-quote
- Supplier Success [supplier_success]: lead onboarding, 2 agent(s) (bd, onboarding), north star: activated suppliers per week
'),
('cat:cleaning','Pricing benchmarks & red flags',7,'Indicative Dubai ranges (always label indicative, confirm per site, all excluding 5% VAT). Hourly maid/general cleaning: roughly AED 25-65 per cleaner-hour, often with a 2-3 hour minimum. Deep cleaning packages: apartments from around AED 299 upward, villas from around AED 599-799+ depending on size and condition; whole-property deep cleans can run several thousand AED for large villas. Time guide: a standard apartment 4-6 hours, a villa 8-10 hours for a full clean. Facade/rope-access and post-construction are quoted per project after a site survey, driven by height, area, access method and permit costs. Price DRIVERS: area and condition, headcount and man-hours, frequency (recurring lowers per-visit cost), access method (rope/cradle far costlier than ground level), consumables and machinery, after-hours/weekend premiums, and certification/insurance overhead. RED FLAGS: a price well below market for the stated area usually means too few hours/cleaners, a ''deep clean'' that is really a surface clean, or uncertified chemicals; facade quotes with no IRATA/cradle certification or no work-at-height permit; no insurance or method statement; VAT and ''extras'' (windows, appliances, balconies) added after booking; day-labour with no supervisor or quality checklist.'),
('_global','Company assets (auto-synced from Dropbox)',60,'COMPANY ASSETS (synced from Dropbox "/QuotesDone Backup/Assets", 147 files). These are real files the company owns; reference them and give the founder the Dropbox path when useful. Folders:
- QuotesDone_Brand_Kit — 58 file(s), 3.2 MB · e.g. AppStore_1024_1024x1024.png, AppleTouch_180_180x180.png, PWA_192_192x192.png, favicon_16_16x16.png, favicon_32_32x32.png, favicon_48_48x48.png
