Text docs (brand voice, colours, letterhead, where-to-use) are read into your knowledge in full. For a specific image/logo/cover, tell the founder the folder above and offer to fetch the exact filename.'),
('cat:facilities-management','Customer intake & scoping questions',9,'1) Property type and size: residential tower, villa community, office, retail/mall, warehouse, mixed-use — and built-up area / number of units? 2) Which services: hard (MEP) only, soft only, or total/integrated FM? 3) Can you share an asset register or list key assets and counts (AC/chillers, AHUs, pumps, electrical panels, lifts, generators, fire systems, BMS)? 4) Comprehensive (spares & consumables included) or non-comprehensive AMC? 5) Required PPM frequency, or shall we propose per asset/manufacturer schedule? 6) SLA expectations: response and resolution times by priority (critical/high/medium/low)? 7) Manning model: resident on-site team (which trades, how many, what shift coverage) or mobile/on-call? 8) Operating hours and need for 24/7 or out-of-hours emergency cover? 9) Compliance scope: fire & life-safety, lift maintenance, water-tank cleaning, statutory inspections required? 10) Soft scope detail: cleaning frequency/areas, security guard count and SIRA needs, pest frequency, landscaping, pool, waste? 11) Reporting: do you need CAFM/helpdesk, KPIs and monthly reports? 12) Contract term and mobilisation date? 13) Existing provider/handover, snags or known issues? 14) Budget or target annual value?'),
('cat:facilities-management','Regulations, licensing & approvals',8,'An FM provider needs a Dubai Department of Economy and Tourism (DET) trade licence covering the relevant FM/maintenance activities, and must comply with the authorities governing each sub-service. Hard services touching water and electricity fall under Dubai Electricity and Water Authority (DEWA) rules, with DEWA-approved contractors and approvals/NOC for works on supply and metering; building MEP works follow the Dubai Building Code and Dubai Municipality (DM) requirements. Fire and life-safety maintenance must meet Dubai Civil Defence (DCD) regulations and the UAE Fire and Life Safety Code of Practice, typically requiring DCD-approved/registered contractors. Lift maintenance and other specialist systems require competent, certified contractors. Soft services carry their own approvals: security guarding requires the company and every guard to be licensed by the Security Industry Regulatory Agency (SIRA); cleaning and waste fall under DM public-health and waste rules; pest control requires DM/MOCCAE compliance (see pest pack). Water-tank cleaning and disinfection are regulated by DM. Staff generally need valid visas, medical fitness/health cards and trade competencies. For owner-association (jointly owned property) buildings, RERA/Mollak service-charge frameworks govern budgeting and provider appointment. Confirm the provider''s licence activities and DCD/SIRA registrations actually cover the scope being quoted.'),
('cat:facilities-management','Standards, materials & specifications',8,'Vocabulary: Hard FM (engineering-led MEP, fabric, fire, lifts) vs Soft FM (cleaning, security, pest, landscaping, waste); PPM = Planned Preventive Maintenance; reactive/corrective maintenance; AMC = Annual Maintenance Contract (comprehensive includes spares/consumables, non-comprehensive excludes them); CAFM = Computer-Aided Facilities Management/helpdesk; SLA = Service Level Agreement (response time to attend, resolution/rectification time to fix); KPI examples — PPM completion rate (target above 98%), SLA adherence, first-time-fix. Guiding standards and codes referenced in UAE practice: the UAE Fire and Life Safety Code of Practice (Dubai Civil Defence) for fire systems; the Dubai Building Code and DM Green Building Regulations for building services and water/energy efficiency; DEWA guidelines for new development and MEP connections; SIRA standards for security personnel. Internationally, ISO 41001 (facility management systems) and ISO 55001 (asset management) are recognised quality frameworks, and manufacturer-recommended service intervals define PPM frequency for chillers, pumps, generators and lifts. Core deliverables: asset register, PPM planner/schedule, RAMS/method statements for risk works, statutory inspection records (fire, lifts, pressure systems, water tanks), and monthly performance reporting against the SLA/KPI matrix.'),
('cat:facilities-management','Pricing benchmarks & red flags',7,'FM is priced per property after a site survey and asset audit, so quote it as a tailored annual value rather than a fixed rate (always label any figure indicative and confirm per site, exclude 5% VAT). Price DRIVERS: total built-up area and asset count; comprehensive vs non-comprehensive (spares can be a large swing); PPM frequency; resident manning (number of trades and shift coverage is usually the biggest cost) vs mobile cover; out-of-hours/24-7 emergency response; specialist sub-systems (chillers, lifts, fire, BMS) and their sub-contracts; SLA stringency and penalty regime; consumables and reporting/CAFM. Soft-service costs scale with cleaning frequency/headcount, number of SIRA-licensed guards and shift pattern, and pest/landscaping frequency. RED FLAGS: a lump-sum AMC with no asset register or PPM schedule; comprehensive cover priced suspiciously low (spares likely excluded and billed later); fewer resident staff than the asset load realistically needs; ''as required'' SLAs with no committed response/resolution times; specialist systems (lifts, fire, BMS) silently out of scope; no out-of-hours or emergency cover; call-out fees, mobilisation and spares as hidden extras; security or fire work quoted without SIRA/DCD registration; KPIs without measurement or penalties; VAT omitted.'),
('cat:facilities-management','Authoritative sources & references',4,'Dubai Department of Economy and Tourism (licensing) — https://www.det.gov.ae/
Dubai Electricity and Water Authority (DEWA) — https://www.dewa.gov.ae/
Dubai Civil Defence (fire & life safety) — https://www.dcd.gov.ae/
Security Industry Regulatory Agency (SIRA) — https://www.sira.gov.ae/
Dubai Municipality (public health, waste, building) — https://www.dm.gov.ae/
Dubai Building Code (DM) — https://www.dm.gov.ae/
RERA / Dubai Land Department (jointly owned property, Mollak) — https://dubailand.gov.ae/
DEWA Guidelines for New Development Projects — https://www.dewa.gov.ae/~/media/Guidelines_For_New_Dev_Projects_Issue_4_Update_2016.ashx
ISO 41001 Facility Management — https://www.iso.org/standard/68021.html
ISO 55001 Asset Management — https://www.iso.org/standard/55089.html
DM Green Building Regulations & Specifications — https://www.dm.gov.ae/wp-content/uploads/2020/11/Green-Buildings-1.pdf'),
('cat:ac-maintenance','Standards, materials & specifications',8,'Energy efficiency/labelling standards: UAE.S 5010-1:2019 (household air conditioners) and UAE.S 5010-5:2019 (commercial & central), enforced via the MOIAT/ESMA EESL scheme with a 5-star energy label and mandatory ECAS Certificate of Conformity from 1 Jan 2021. Capacity is rated in tons of refrigeration (TR) or BTU/hr (1 TR ~ 12,000 BTU/hr); efficiency in SEER/EER and star rating (5 stars highest).

Refrigerants: R410A and R32 are the common modern HFCs — they are not interchangeable and must not be mixed; older R22 is being phased out under the Montreal Protocol. Equipment types: window, non-ducted split, ducted/concealed split, cassette, package unit, VRF/VRV, and central chiller systems. Compressors: rotary or twin-rotary inverter. Typical new-unit warranty: 1-year full unit plus 5-year compressor. Common brands: Daikin, O General, Carrier, Gree, LG, Samsung, Trane, Taqeef/Super General.

Maintenance vocabulary: tonnage, BTU, SEER/EER, refrigerant charge, superheat/subcool, condenser coil, evaporator coil, drain pan/condensate line, capacitor, PCB, thermostat, AMC (annual maintenance contract), preventive visit.'),
('cat:pest-control','Quote analysis & comparison',9,'A complete pest-control quote states: target pest(s) (cockroaches, ants, rodents, bed bugs, termites, mosquitoes, flies, stored-product pests); property type and size (studio/BR count/villa/sqft, or commercial area); treatment TYPE and method (gel baiting, residual spray, fogging/ULV/thermal fog, dusting, rodent bait stations/snap traps, termite soil treatment or baiting system, bed-bug chemical vs heat treatment); number of VISITS and schedule (one-off, follow-ups, monthly/quarterly contract); the specific chemicals/products and that they are MOCCAE-registered and DM-approved, with MSDS; whether a DM-approved certificate is issued per visit (essential for restaurants/food premises and trade-licence renewals); technician certification; warranty/free re-treatment period; and 5% VAT. STRONG QUOTE: named registered chemicals, treatment type matched to pest and biology, realistic visit count (bed bugs and termites rarely resolve in one visit), DM certificate included, warranty. UNDERPRICED/CORNER-CUTTING and WHICH corner: too few visits for the infestation (single bed-bug or termite spray that won''t clear it); cheap broad-spectrum spray instead of correct targeted method; uncertified/unregistered chemicals or untrained technicians; no DM certificate (then commercial client fails inspection). HIGHLIGHT TO CUSTOMER: treatment method and visit count vs pest type, whether DM-approved certificate is included, chemical registration, warranty/re-treatment, contract vs one-off value. TRICKS: ''general spray'' with no pest target, follow-ups billed extra, no certificate, vague chemical names, VAT added later, no guarantee.'),
('cat:pest-control','Customer intake & scoping questions',9,'1) Which pest(s) are you dealing with: cockroaches, ants, bed bugs, rodents, termites, mosquitoes/flies, or general prevention? 2) Property type and size: studio/1/2/3+BR apartment, townhouse, villa (and plot for termites), office, restaurant/kitchen, warehouse, plus approximate sqft? 3) Is this residential or commercial — and do you need a Dubai Municipality-approved certificate (required for restaurants/food premises and trade-licence renewal)? 4) Severity: first sighting, recurring, or heavy/visible infestation, and how long has it been present? 5) One-off treatment or an ongoing contract (monthly/quarterly/annual)? 6) For bed bugs: do you prefer chemical treatment or heat treatment, and how many rooms/beds affected? 7) For termites: pre-construction soil treatment, post-construction, or a baiting system, and is there visible structural damage? 8) Is the property occupied (children, elderly, pets, food businesses) so we choose low-toxicity/appropriate products and timing? 9) Any prior treatments and did they work? 10) Access, vacancy period needed after treatment, and preferred timing (after-hours for restaurants)? 11) Do you need a warranty/free re-treatment period? 12) Budget or target price per visit/contract?'),
('cat:pest-control','Regulations, licensing & approvals',8,'Two authorities govern pest control in Dubai. The Dubai Department of Economy and Tourism (DET/DED) issues the mainland trade licence for Public Health Pests Control Services (activity code 8129.92). Dubai Municipality (Public Health & Safety Department) issues the OPERATIONAL PERMIT that must be in place before any pest-control work begins, and regulates the practice. Both the DET licence and the DM operational permit require annual renewal. Technicians must hold a permit to practise, pass a competency/qualification test, and have approved health certificates and valid residency/sponsorship; supervisors/managers typically need a relevant degree (Agriculture, Environmental Science, Chemistry or similar) or a WHO-affiliated training certificate and to pass the qualification test (commonly 70% pass mark). Pesticides must be REGISTERED with the UAE Ministry of Climate Change and Environment (MOCCAE) before they can be imported or circulated — registration is generally valid up to five years, and import requires MOCCAE permits; only registered, DM-approved products may be used. For commercial food premises (restaurants, kitchens), each treatment should produce a DM-approved pest-control certificate used in health inspections and licence renewal. Setting up a compliant company typically takes around 4-8 weeks subject to DED and DM approvals. Always confirm the contractor holds both a valid DET licence and current DM permit, and uses registered chemicals applied by permitted technicians.'),
('cat:pest-control','Standards, materials & specifications',8,'Vocabulary and methods by target. Crawling insects (cockroaches/ants): gel baiting, residual/perimeter spraying, dusting of voids. Rodents: tamper-resistant bait stations, snap/glue traps, proofing of entry points (Integrated Pest Management favours exclusion over poison). Flying insects/mosquitoes: ULV cold fogging, thermal fogging outdoors, larviciding of standing water, insect light traps indoors. Bed bugs: targeted chemical (residual + crack-and-crevice) usually needing repeat visits, or heat/thermal treatment raising room temperature to lethal levels in one cycle. Termites: pre-construction soil/anti-termite treatment, post-construction drilling and chemical injection, or in-ground baiting systems for ongoing colony control. Stored-product pests and food areas use IPM with monitoring. All products must be MOCCAE-registered and DM-approved, applied per the label, with MSDS and a treatment record. Best practice is Integrated Pest Management (IPM): inspection, identification, monitoring, targeted treatment, proofing/sanitation advice, and verification. For commercial/food premises, expect a documented pest-management programme, monitoring logbook, service report per visit, and the DM-approved certificate. Technicians must be DM-permitted; PPE and safe re-entry/aeration times should be specified, especially for fogging and occupied premises.'),
('cat:pest-control','Pricing benchmarks & red flags',7,'Indicative Dubai ranges (label indicative, confirm per site, exclude 5% VAT). One-off residential general treatment: roughly AED 140-150 for a studio up to around AED 500-700 for a large villa. Cockroach treatment: roughly AED 150-300 depending on size. Bed bugs: chemical spray from around AED 500 per bedroom (usually multiple visits), heat treatment roughly AED 1,200-2,500 per standard bedroom. Termites: villa soil treatment roughly AED 800-1,500, full baiting/extensive structural treatment AED 3,000-5,000+. Annual residential contracts commonly fall around AED 999-3,000 depending on property and pests. Commercial/restaurant work is usually a monthly contract for DM compliance, priced on area and risk. Price DRIVERS: pest type and severity, property size, method (heat treatment and termite baiting cost far more than spraying), number of visits/follow-ups, contract vs one-off (contracts cut per-visit cost), and whether a DM certificate and warranty are included. RED FLAGS: a price far below market usually means too few visits, a generic spray instead of the correct method, or unregistered/uncertified chemicals and technicians; no DM-approved certificate for a commercial client; ''guaranteed one-visit'' cure for bed bugs or termites; follow-ups and certificate billed as hidden extras; no warranty; vague or unnamed chemicals; VAT added after quoting.'),
('cat:pest-control','Authoritative sources & references',4,'UAE Ministry of Climate Change and Environment (MOCCAE) — Pesticide registration — https://www.moccae.gov.ae/en/services/registration-accreditation/registration-of-a-pesticide.aspx
MOCCAE — Registered products & materials — https://www.moccae.gov.ae/en/our-services/registered-products-and-materials.aspx
MOCCAE — Import a pesticide consignment — https://www.moccae.gov.ae/en/services/permit-of-pesticide-import
Dubai Municipality — Technical Guidelines List — https://www.dm.gov.ae/municipality-business/technical-guidelines-list/
Dubai Municipality (main portal) — https://www.dm.gov.ae/
Dubai Department of Economy and Tourism (trade licence, activity 8129.92) — https://www.det.gov.ae/
Meydan Free Zone — Public Health Pests Control Services licence (activity 8129.92) — https://www.meydanfz.ae/activity-hub/apply-for-a-public-health-pests-control-services-license-in-dubai
Dubai Municipality Pest Control guidelines (unified municipal requirements) — https://www.scribd.com/document/686121971/Unified-Municipal-Guide-Requirements-for-Pest-Control
AltPests — Pest control cost guide Dubai 2026 (indicative pricing) — https://altpests.com/blog/pest-control-cost-dubai-2026/
Gulf News — New rules on pesticide registration issued — https://gulfnews.com/uae/environment/new-rules-on-pesticide-registration-issued-1.2170545'),
('cat:plumbing','Quote analysis & comparison',9,'A complete plumbing quote states whether it is SUPPLY-ONLY, install-only, or supply-and-install; the scope (repair, fixture/heater install, re-piping, drainage, leak detection, blockage clearing, full fit-out); pipe MATERIAL and pressure rating (PPR or CPVC for hot/cold water with PN class such as PN16/PN20/PN25 indicating bar rating; uPVC for drainage; copper where used) plus fittings; fixture make/model (mixers, WCs, water heaters by brand/capacity such as 50/80/100L); labour and any call-out fee; whether DEWA approval/NOC and water-efficient fittings are needed; testing (pressure test on supply, leak test); reinstatement (ceiling/wall/tile cut-and-make-good); warranty on parts and labour; and 5% VAT. STRONG QUOTE: named pipe material and PN class, branded fixtures with model and capacity, pressure-testing included, make-good included, parts+labour warranty, DEWA NOC where applicable. UNDERPRICED/CORNER-CUTTING and WHICH corner: cheaper/thinner pipe or lower PN class than the application needs; unbranded or lower-capacity fixtures/heater; supply-only quote compared against supply-and-install (omits material cost); no pressure test; ceiling/wall make-good excluded then billed; uncertified plumber on DEWA-related work. HIGHLIGHT TO CUSTOMER: supply-and-install vs supply-only, exact pipe material/PN class, fixture brand and capacity, whether testing and reinstatement are included, warranty length, DEWA approval responsibility. TRICKS: low headline price excluding materials, call-out fee not credited to the job, make-good and disposal extra, vague ''as required'' fittings, VAT and access/ceiling works hidden.'),
('cat:handyman','Standards, materials & specifications',8,'Core scope vocabulary: AMC (Annual Maintenance Contract), PPM (planned preventive maintenance), SLA (service level agreement / response time), reactive/breakdown calls, snag/snagging, callout/minimum charge. AC servicing: filter and coil cleaning, drainage/condensate line flush, gas/refrigerant top-up (R410A/R32), thermostat check, fan-coil unit (FCU) and air-handling unit (AHU) service; ducted split, window/wall split, and central chilled-water systems differ in scope. Electrical: DEWA-registered contractor required for fixed wiring; works should follow the DEWA wiring/electrical installation regulations and UAE codes; RCD/MCB, distribution board (DB), earthing. Plumbing: PPR/PEX pipework, water heater (geyser) descaling and anode, pressure-reducing valve, water-tank cleaning (often a Dubai Municipality-regulated activity for tanks). Painting: surface prep, primer, two-coat finish, paint brand/sheen. Fire and life safety upkeep falls under the UAE Fire and Life Safety Code where applicable. Quality markers: named part brands, manufacturer-recommended service intervals, calibrated tools, and certified technicians. A good AMC schedule states visit frequency, a task checklist per visit, consumables included (filters/sealant), parts policy with a cap, and measurable SLA response times for emergencies (e.g. 4 hours, 24/7 hotline).'),
('cat:handyman','Pricing benchmarks & red flags',7,'Indicative Dubai pricing (verify; varies by scope and property): per-visit handyman call-outs commonly carry a minimum charge of roughly AED 100-250 before materials; hourly skilled labour often ~AED 80-200/hr. AC split-unit service indicatively ~AED 100-250 per unit (gas top-up extra). Residential AMC packages are commonly tiered by apartment size/villa - indicative apartment AMC ~AED 1,000-3,500/year and villa AMC higher (verify), priced by number of visits, systems covered, and whether parts are included. Drivers: number of preventive visits, systems and units covered (AC unit count especially), parts-included cap vs labour-only, emergency SLA (24/7 commands a premium), and property size. Red flags: an unusually cheap AMC headline that turns out labour-only with every part and AC gas billed extra; vague ''unlimited visits'' with no defined SLA or task checklist; no DET activity match or no DEWA registration for electrical work; freelance/unsponsored technicians; no written workmanship warranty; large upfront full-year payment with no service schedule; and refusal to itemise labour vs materials. For one-offs, watch undisclosed minimum/callout charges and missing 5% VAT.'),
('cat:movers','Regulations, licensing & approvals',8,'A Dubai moving company requires a dual setup: a Dubai Department of Economy and Tourism (DET) trade licence for the moving/freight/relocation activity, plus Roads and Transport Authority (RTA) authorisation for operating commercial goods vehicles - the RTA No Objection Certificate (NOC) / operation card must be obtained and is typically secured before the DET trade licence is finalised. Moving trucks must be commercially registered with the RTA under the company name with valid goods-transport permits; a trade licence alone does not entitle a firm to run moving trucks. Crew (packers and drivers) must be on the company''s MOHRE-sponsored visas with valid Emirates IDs - unsponsored or ''freelance'' labour is non-compliant. Buildings and gated communities (Emaar, Nakheel, Meraas, etc.) often require a move-in/move-out NOC or permit, a booked service-lift slot, and a refundable security deposit; some communities mandate approved movers. International moves add customs/freight documentation. For comparing quotes, confirm the mover holds a valid DET licence AND RTA permits, employs its own visa-sponsored crew, and can arrange the building/community access permit. Verify goods-in-transit insurance is placed with a UAE-registered insurer (indicatively a minimum around AED 100,000 of cover is referenced for licensed movers - verify per provider).'),
('cat:plumbing','Customer intake & scoping questions',9,'1) What is the job: a repair (leak/blockage), a fixture install/replacement (tap, WC, sink, shower, water heater), drainage work, full re-piping, or a new fit-out? 2) Property type: apartment, villa, townhouse, office, retail, warehouse — and is it tenanted/occupied? 3) For installs: do you want supply-and-install, or will you supply the fixtures/heater (and what brand, model, capacity e.g. 50/80/100L)? 4) Where is the issue and how many points/fixtures or bathrooms are involved? 5) For leaks: do you know the source, or is leak detection needed (and is it behind tiles/in a slab/ceiling)? 6) Any structural access needed — ceiling, wall or floor opening — and do you want make-good (tiling/plaster/paint) included? 7) Does the work touch DEWA supply, the meter, or main drainage (which may need DEWA approval/NOC)? 8) Required pipe material/standard or water-efficient fittings (green-building compliance)? 9) Is this urgent/emergency or scheduled, and any access/timing constraints? 10) Do you need a warranty on parts and labour, and for how long? 11) Should a pressure/leak test and commissioning be included and certified? 12) Single job or a maintenance contract? 13) Budget or target price?'),
('cat:curtains-blinds','Quote analysis & comparison',9,'A complete curtains/blinds quote must itemise: window-by-window measurements (W x H, mm), product type per window (wave/pinch-pleat/eyelet curtains; roller/zebra/roman/venetian/honeycomb blinds), fabric name + composition + colour/collection, lining (blackout/dimout/sheer/thermal), track or rod (corded vs motorised), motor brand and model if automated, accessories (tiebacks, valance, pelmet, brackets, side channels), supply vs supply-and-install, removal of old window coverings, delivery, warranty, and VAT (5%). Differentiating spec parameters WITH units: fabric weight (gsm), light-blockout grade (e.g. 100% blackout vs dimout), motor brand/model (Somfy Irismo/Glydea, vs generic/Tuya), control (RF remote, wired wall switch, app/Alexa/Google, hub required?), track quality (aluminium vs plastic, bendable for bay windows), fabric width (railroaded to avoid seams), and FR certification (NFPA 701 / BS 5867 Part 2 Type B). Strong quote: named fabric with batch FR test certificate, branded Somfy motor with model and 5-yr warranty, full install + old-curtain removal included, fixed measurements not ''approx''. Corner-cutting signs: ''motorised'' without naming the motor (generic motor relabelled), no FR certificate where code requires it, supply-only disguised as a full price, thin/low-gsm fabric, plastic track sold as aluminium, ''per window'' price hiding stitching/lining as extras. Highlight to a customer comparing 2-3 quotes: is the motor genuinely Somfy, is blackout actually 100%, are measurement/install/removal and VAT all included, and is warranty on motor AND fabric. Common tricks: ''starting from'' per-sqm rates, free-install offers that load fabric price, and excluded scaffolding for high/double-height windows.')
on conflict do nothing;
