('_global','Asset text: 05_FIXES_APPLIED_2026-07-19.md',45,'From company asset "05_FIXES_APPLIED_2026-07-19.md" (Dropbox: /QuotesDone Backup/Assets/05_FIXES_APPLIED_2026-07-19.md)

# QuotesDone OS — Remediation Pass (2026-07-19)

Applied against live production `htdblwldjkcgjvmqkbeh` ("suppliers DB") via the connected Supabase
management interface. Method followed CLAUDE.md discipline: pulled live ground truth first (the archive is
a snapshot; production is the truth), applied only safe/reversible changes, and verified each with a
before/after query. Nothing was applied that I could not verify from here.

---

## ✅ Applied to production and verified

### C1 — Supplier-identity view leak (CRITICAL) — CLOSED
`v_supplier_billing`, `v_llm_model_mix`, `v_llm_offpolicy`, `v_kb_regulation_gaps` were readable by the
`anon` and `authenticated` roles and bypassed RLS (not `security_invoker`). `v_supplier_billing` exposed
supplier company name, email, ban status and subscription state to anyone holding the public anon key.

- **Action:** `REVOKE ALL ... FROM anon, authenticated` on all four (migration `c1_close_anon_view_leak_20260719`).
- **Safety confirmed first:** both internal callers (`fn_subscription_billing_run`, `fn_llm_drift_watch`) are
  SECURITY DEFINER (owner-run, RLS-bypassing) and retain access via `service_role`.
- **Proof after:** `has_table_privilege` for anon = **false**, authenticated = **false**, service_role = **true**, all four.

### H3 — Unauthenticated RPC surface (HIGH) — CLOSED
20 SECURITY DEFINER functions were EXECUTE-able by `anon`/`authenticated` — including `fn_os_list`/`fn_os_detail`
(generic entity accessors the 2026-07-07 patch missed) and `fn_subscription_billing_run` (mutates billing).

- **Trap caught:** EXECUTE was held via a **PUBLIC** grant *and* explicit anon/authenticated grants — revoking
  only anon/authenticated would have left the hole open via PUBLIC. Revoked from **PUBLIC + anon + authenticated**.
- **Action:** migration `h3_revoke_anon_execute_secdef_rpcs_20260719`.
- **Proof after:** SECURITY DEFINER functions still anon/authenticated-executable = **0**; `service_role`
  retains EXECUTE (edge functions unaffected).

### H4 — Anon exposure root cause + defense-in-depth (HIGH) — CLOSED
Supabase''s platform defaults grant every new `public` object to anon/authenticated (this is why the C1 views
were born exposed), and 90 existing tables carried blanket anon grants (inert only under deny-all RLS).

- **H4a (default privileges):** `ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES FOR ROLE postgres IN SCHEMA public REVOKE ALL ... FROM anon, authenticated`
  (tables/functions/sequences). Future-only — cannot affect existing objects. **Proof:** a throwaway
  `postgres`-created table returned anon=false/authenticated=false/service_role=true. (The `supabase_admin`
  default needs a superuser; noted — it governs Supabase-internal objects, not your app objects.)
- **H4b (existing grants):** revoked anon/authenticated on all existing public tables except `category_specs`.
  **Proof:** anon/authenticated-readable public tables **90 → 1** (only `category_specs`); service_role still
  reads `leads`/`quotes`.
- **Net:** RLS is now the *second* barrier, not the only one — a future mistaken policy or non-invoker view
  can no longer expose a table that has no explicit grant.

### C2 (core) — Deploy pipeline would ship a 404 body as the console — DEFUSED
`site_deploy`''s `MAIN` map fetches `/functions/v1/os2` and `/os3`, but both page-server functions had been
deleted, and the render loop has no `res.ok` check — so the next deploy would upload a Supabase 404 body as
`/os2` and `/os3` (and `/admin`, `/crm` redirect to `/os3`).

- **Action:** recreated the `os2` and `os3` page-server edge functions (exact clones of the working `app`
  function, `verify_jwt=false`, reading `pages.slug=''os2''`/`''os3''`). Confirmed `os3` serves the 93 KB console.
- **Result:** the next `site_deploy` now renders the console correctly. No production deploy was triggered by
  this (creating functions doesn''t mark the site dirty), so the live site is unchanged and safe.

---

**Final verified posture:** 0 anon-readable views · 0 anon-executable SECURITY DEFINER functions · 1 anon-readable table (`category_specs`) · 0 anon-granting default privileges.

## 🟡 Staged — ready, deliberately not applied (see `QuotesDone/docs/upgrade/hotfix-2026-07-19/`)

| Item | Why staged, not auto-applied |
|---|---|
| **C2 guard** (`site_deploy.guarded.ts`) | Redeploying the 30 KB deploy function from here is riskier than the residual it removes (the live failure mode is already inactive). Deploy from your checkout: `supabase functions deploy site_deploy`. |
| **H1** portal `quote_ai` 404 | **No drop-in fix** — `supplier_api.extract_quote` needs images and can''t take pasted text. Needs a product decision (extend `quote_extract` for text / recreate `quote_ai` / hide the paste box). 0 registered suppliers = no live impact today. |
| **M1** os3 XSS escaping | Editing `pages.os3` auto-triggers a production deploy I can''t fully verify from here; not live-exploitable now (needs planted content). Exact edit documented. |

**Key operational finding:** editing any `pages`/`blog_posts`/`seo_categories` row flips `site_publish_state.dirty`
and an active cron (`site_autodeploy`, every minute) runs a full production Vercel deploy within ~1 minute. That
is why the page-level fixes (H1, M1) are staged rather than slipped in — each one ships to production automatically,
and the 90 KB+ pages exceed what I can fetch-verify from this environment.

---

## 🔴 Needs you

- **H2 — `stripe_webhook_secret` is unset**, so Stripe webhooks fail closed (rejected). I cannot supply the
  value; set it in `app_secrets` from your Stripe dashboard signing secret, then send a test event and confirm
  in `agent_runs`. Launch blocker for the reveal-fee / fine loop.

## 🔵 Roadmap (bigger, not this pass)

- **C3** — one `inbound_secret` authorises ~128 functions incl. self-confirmable high-risk actions; `os_api`
  also accepts a static `x-os-key`. Split by domain, require MFA on the console path, remove the bypass.
- **Git mirror + nightly drift gate** — the single highest-leverage change. C1 existed because nothing watches
  view/grant drift. A nightly advisor + grant-diff with an alert would have caught it the day it appeared.

---

## Rollback

Both applied migrations are reversible; rollback SQL is in `01_c1_*.APPLIED.sql` and `02_h3_*.APPLIED.sql`
(commented). The two new page-server functions can be deleted with no effect on anything but the (now-safe)
deploy render. No data was modified; no production deploy was triggered.

---

# Remediation Pass 2 (same day) — "finish everything"

More applied live and verified:

## ✅ Applied + verified
- **Drift gate (the durable preventive).** Extended the existing daily `fn_security_selfaudit` (cron
  `security_selfaudit_daily`) to detect and alert — via `founder_alerts` + `security_events` — if the
  anonymity-by-construction invariant ever regresses (anon-readable views/tables, anon-executable SECURITY
  DEFINER functions, anon default privileges). **Verified `drift = []` today.** This is what makes a
  C1-class bug self-announcing instead of silent. Snapshot columns `anon_tables/anon_views/anon_defaults` added.
- **H4a (root cause).** Revoked anon/authenticated default privileges for role `postgres` in `public` — new
  dashboard/SQL-editor objects are no longer born anon-readable. Verified on a throwaway table
  (anon=false/auth=false/service_role=true).
- **H4b (defense-in-depth).** Revoked blanket anon/authenticated grants on existing public tables: **90 → 1**
  (only `category_specs`). service_role retained (leads/quotes readable).
- **H1 — `quote_ai` recreated** as a text-mode anonymising extractor (mirrors `quote_extract`, same output
  shape, supplier-authed, CORS). Portal paste-quote path no longer 404s. Verified: no-auth call → **401**
  with the custom message (proves the deployed code + auth gate). No page edit / no auto-deploy.

## ✅ Res'),
('cat:handyman','Regulations, licensing & approvals',8,'A Dubai maintenance/handyman company needs a Dubai Department of Economy and Tourism (DET) trade licence for the relevant activity - typically ''Building Cleaning & Maintenance'' / ''Technical Services'' or trade-specific activities (electromechanical, AC maintenance, plumbing, electrical works) - plus a registered office and Ejari tenancy. Companies offering HVAC, electrical or plumbing usually need additional approvals from Dubai Municipality, and electrical work must be carried out by a DEWA-registered/approved contractor (DEWA controls connection to its network and registers electrical contractors by grade). Technicians should be on the company''s MOHRE-sponsored visas with Emirates IDs. In managed buildings and communities, work commonly requires an owners-association/master-developer NOC and use of an approved-contractor list, and larger fit-out or structural works require Dubai Municipality (or free-zone authority e.g. Trakhees, DDA) building permits and DEWA approvals. AC refrigerant handling and disposal is regulated; reputable firms follow environmental rules. Verify the contractor''s DET licence covers the exact activity, that electrical staff are DEWA-registered, and that for community properties the firm is on the approved list or can obtain the NOC. Avoid unlicensed individual ''freelance'' handymen for electrical or gas work - it can void insurance and breach building rules.'),
('cat:movers','Customer intake & scoping questions',9,'Ask: (1) Move type - local within Dubai/UAE, inter-emirate, or international? (2) Origin and destination full addresses, property type (studio/1-2-3 BR apartment, villa) and number of rooms? (3) Floor and access at both ends - lift available or stairs only, distance from truck parking, narrow corridors? (4) Approximate volume - rooms/CBM - and can you do a video or in-home survey? (5) Packing level - full packing by the crew, partial (kitchen/fragile only), or you pack? (6) Do you need furniture dismantling and reassembly (beds, wardrobes, modular kitchen)? (7) Appliance handling - AC uninstall/reinstall, washing machine, TV wall-mount removal/fitting? (8) Any special/high-value items - piano, safe, large marble table, chandelier, artwork, gym equipment? (9) Do you require insurance (goods-in-transit / all-risk) and at what declared value? (10) Storage needed between move-out and move-in (how long, climate-controlled)? (11) Preferred moving date and any time slot fixed by the building/community (move-in permit, security deposit)? (12) Is a community/RTA gate pass or building NOC required at either end? (13) Any items to be disposed of or donated? (14) Budget expectation and is this a tenant or owner move?'),
('cat:customer_success','Field expertise — UAE codes, standards & specs',8,'Customer Success in the UAE context refers to professional services — consulting, software platforms, managed services, and outsourced teams — that help businesses reduce churn, drive product adoption, and grow account revenue. It is a commercial and technology services category, not a licensed trade in the traditional regulatory sense, meaning there is no single UAE regulator issuing permits specifically for "Customer Success." However, several compliance layers apply depending on delivery model.

**Governing Authority & Approvals:** Any entity providing Customer Success services commercially in Dubai must hold a valid trade licence from the Department of Economy and Tourism (DET) under an appropriate activity code — typically Management Consultancy, IT Consultancy, or Business Process Outsourcing. Free-zone operators (DIFC, DMCC, Dubai Internet City, Dubai Silicon Oasis) require a free-zone licence and must respect onshore/offshore client engagement rules. If the service involves handling personal customer data — CRM records, usage analytics, NPS responses — compliance with UAE Federal Law on Personal Data Protection (PDPL) is mandatory, overseen by the UAE Data Office. Firms operating in DIFC fall under the DIFC Data Protection Law, enforced by the DIFC Commissioner of Data Protection. SaaS platforms used for Customer Success (Gainsight, Salesforce, ChurnZero, Totango) must store UAE customer data in accordance with PDPL data-residency guidance. If the scope includes call-centre or contact-centre operations, a Telecommunications and Digital Government Regulatory Authority (TDRA) registration may be required.

**Standards:** ISO 9001 (quality management) and ISO/IEC 27001 (information security) are the most commercially relevant certifications buyers request. ITIL frameworks govern service-management processes. NPS, CSAT, and CES are the accepted measurement standards industry-wide.

**Scope & Specs:** Key deliverables include onboarding programme design, health-score modelling, QBR (Quarterly Business Review) cadences, renewal and upsell playbooks, CSM-to-account ratios (typically 1:10 enterprise, 1:50 commercial), churn-risk dashboards, and VOC (Voice of Customer) programmes.

**Price Drivers:** Team seniority and CSM headcount, platform licensing fees, integration complexity with existing CRM/ERP, SLA response tiers, language requirements (Arabic localisation adds cost), and whether delivery is onshore or nearshore.

**Compliance Pitfalls / Red FLAGS:** Unlicensed activity if consultancy scope creeps beyond the stated DET licence activity; PDPL breach if CRM data is processed on servers outside UAE without adequate safeguards; misclassification of outsourced CSMs as independent contractors rather than employees under UAE Labour Law (Federal Decree-Law on Labour Relations). Sharp qualifying questions: What CRM/CS platform is currently in use and where is data hosted? Is Arabic-language support required? Are CSMs to be employed, contracted, or fully outsourced? What is the current churn rate and ARR at risk? Is DIFC or onshore law governing the client contract?'),
('cat:ai_trainer','Field expertise — UAE codes, standards & specs',8,'AI Trainer services in the UAE operate as a professional knowledge-services category rather than a physical installation trade, meaning the regulatory landscape differs substantially from construction or engineering trades. The primary governing framework falls under the UAE''s broader digital economy and data governance architecture. The UAE Artificial Intelligence Office, operating under the Ministry of AI, Digital Economy and Remote Work Applications, sets national AI strategy and may require alignment with the UAE National AI Strategy 2031 for government-facing projects. For data handling integral to AI training, the UAE Federal Data Protection Law (Federal Decree-Law No. 45 of 2021) governs personal data processing, and the Dubai International Financial Centre''s DIFC Data Protection Law applies within that free zone. Abu Dhabi Global Market has its own data protection framework. Trainers working with government entities may need to comply with UAE Cybersecurity Council guidelines and the National Cybersecurity Strategy. Free zone establishment (DIFC, ADGM, Dubai Internet City, Dubai Silicon Oasis) typically requires a professional services trade licence from the relevant free zone authority rather than a mainland DED licence, though both are valid routes.

Applicable standards include ISO/IEC 42001 (AI management systems), ISO/IEC 27001 (information security management), and ISO/IEC 27701 (privacy information management), all increasingly referenced in UAE enterprise procurement. GDPR alignment is often contractually required for multinational clients.

Core scope covers dataset curation and annotation, model fine-tuning (LLMs, computer vision, NLP), RLHF (Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback), prompt engineering, evaluation benchmarking, bias auditing, and MLOps pipeline integration. Buyers discuss GPU compute hours, annotation accuracy rates, inter-annotator agreement scores, model performance metrics (F1, BLEU, ROUGE), and data labelling tooling (Label Studio, Scale AI, CVAT).

Price drivers include dataset volume and complexity, annotation language (Arabic NLP commands premium rates), domain specialisation (legal, medical, financial), compute infrastructure costs, and turnaround timelines.

Critical red flags: vague data ownership clauses, absence of NDA or DPA agreements, no mention of UAE data residency requirements, unlicensed freelancers handling sensitive government data, and failure to specify model evaluation benchmarks pre-delivery. Key qualifying questions: Who owns the training data and output model weights? Is personal data involved, and where will it be stored? Is the end client a UAE government entity requiring specific security clearance? What annotation languages and domains are required? What compute environment is mandated?'),
('cat:security_ai','Field expertise — UAE codes, standards & specs',8,'Security AI in the UAE encompasses AI-powered surveillance, threat detection, access control analytics, and behavioural intelligence systems deployed across commercial, residential, hospitality, and critical infrastructure sites. The field sits at the intersection of physical security hardware and intelligent software, making regulatory compliance multi-layered.

**Governing Authority & Approvals**

The primary regulator for security systems in Dubai is SIRA (Security Industry Regulatory Agency), which operates under Dubai Police. Any entity supplying, installing, or operating AI-driven CCTV, video analytics, or access control systems in Dubai must hold a valid SIRA licence — both the company and individual technicians. Dubai Police''s Command and Control Centre sets integration requirements for systems connected to city-wide surveillance networks. For Abu Dhabi, the equivalent authority is the Abu Dhabi Police / Critical Infrastructure and Coastal Protection Authority (CICPA) for sensitive sites. Free-zone installations (Jebel Ali, DIFC, Dubai Airport Free Zone) fall under Trakhees or the relevant free-zone authority, which may require parallel NOC submissions alongside SIRA. Dubai Municipality approval is required where structural modifications accompany installations. For AI systems processing personal biometric data (facial recognition, gait analysis), compliance with the UAE Federal Personal Data Protection Law (Federal Decree-Law No. 45 of 2021) is mandatory, with the UAE Data Office as the oversight body. Government and critical national infrastructure deployments may require additional clearance from the Signals Intelligence Agency (SIGINT/NSA-equivalent) or the National Electronic Security Authority (NESA), now operating under UAE Cybersecurity Council frameworks.

**Technical Standards**

Relevant standards include IEC 62676 series (video surveillance systems for use in security applications — covers IP cameras, recording, and transmission), ONVIF conformance profiles (Profile S, T, G) for interoperability, ISO/IEC 27001 for information security management of AI platforms, NDAA Section 889 compliance (prohibiting certain Chinese-manufactured components on US-aligned government contracts, increasingly referenced in UAE federal tenders), and EN 50132 series for CCTV. For access control integration, Wiegand, OSDP, and RS-485 protocol standards are commonly specified. SIRA''s own Code of Practice for Electronic Security Systems is the binding local technical reference and supersedes generic international standards where they conflict. Cabling infrastructure must comply with TIA/EIA structured cabling standards and, where power is involved, DEWA''s Wiring Regulations (based on IEC 60364).

**Scope & Specifications**

Core components include AI-enabled IP cameras (fixed, PTZ, fisheye) with edge-processing SoCs (Ambarella, Hisilicon, NVIDIA Jetson at the edge), Video Management Software (VMS) platforms (Milestone, Genetec, Avigilon, Dahua DSS, Hikvision HikCentral), AI analytics engines for facial recognition, crowd density estimation, perimeter intrusion detection, licence plate recognition (LPR/ANPR), object classification, and behavioural anomaly detection. Access control layers'),
('cat:legal_ai','Field expertise — UAE codes, standards & specs',8,'Legal AI in the UAE encompasses AI-powered tools for contract analysis, legal research, document drafting, due diligence, compliance monitoring, and litigation support. The market is growing rapidly given Dubai''s ambition to become a global legal hub, with DIFC Courts, ADGM Courts, and onshore UAE courts all generating significant demand.

**Governing Authority & Approvals**

Legal AI deployments in the UAE sit across multiple regulatory layers. The UAE Personal Data Protection Law (Federal Decree-Law No. 45 of 2021) governs how Legal AI systems process personal data, requiring data minimisation, purpose limitation, and cross-border transfer controls. DIFC has its own Data Protection Law (DIFC Law No. 5 of 2020) enforced by the DIFC Commissioner of Data Protection, and ADGM operates under its own Data Protection Regulations — both stricter than the federal baseline. The UAE AI Office (under the Ministry of AI) sets national AI strategy and may issue guidance affecting Legal AI procurement in government contexts. For Legal AI used by licensed law firms, the Dubai Legal Affairs Department and the UAE Ministry of Justice oversee legal practice licensing; AI tools cannot constitute unauthorised legal practice. DIFC-registered law firms answer to the DIFC Authority. Any SaaS Legal AI platform storing data in-country must consider UAE data residency expectations, particularly for government or quasi-government clients.

**Standards**

ISO/IEC 42001 (AI Management Systems) is the primary international standard buyers reference for responsible AI governance. ISO/IEC 27001 (Information Security Management) is routinely required in procurement RFPs. ISO/IEC 27701 (Privacy Information Management) is increasingly demanded given data protection obligations. Suppliers should also reference the UAE National AI Ethics Guidelines and DIFC''s published AI governance guidance. For Legal AI handling court documents, chain-of-custody and audit-trail requirements align with ISO 15489 (records management).

**Scope & Specs**

Core components buyers evaluate: contract lifecycle management (CLM) modules, natural language processing (NLP) engines (Arabic and English bilingual capability is critical), large language model (LLM) integration (GPT-4-class or equivalent), optical character recognition (OCR) for Arabic-script documents, clause extraction and risk-scoring engines, regulatory change monitoring feeds (UAE Official Gazette, DIFC/ADGM rulebooks), e-signature integration (compliant with UAE Federal Law on Electronic Transactions), matter management and billing integration, and role-based access controls. Arabic NLP quality is a primary differentiator — many global platforms underperform on Gulf Arabic and formal Modern Standard Arabic legal terminology. Deployment options discussed: cloud (AWS/Azure Middle East regions for data residency), on-premise, or hybrid. API connectivity to document management systems (iManage, NetDocuments) and ERP/legal billing platforms (Clio, Aderant) is commonly specified.

**Price Drivers**

Pricing is driven by: number of users/seats, volume of contracts processed monthly, Arabic NLP capability premium, custom model fine-tuning on client-specific clause libraries, data residency (on-'),
